"url":"/wiki/clients#client-implementations","content":"xunit (tests)"},"5651":{"id":"/wiki/clients-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#client-implementations","content":"Java"},"5652":{"id":"/wiki/clients-38","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#client-implementations","content":"`booblik-java` on reposilite"},"5653":{"id":"/wiki/clients-39","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#client-implementations","content":"producer, consumer"},"5654":{"id":"/wiki/clients-40","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#client-implementations","content":"JUnit (tests)"},"5655":{"id":"/wiki/clients-41","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#client-implementations","content":"Kotlin/Native"},"5656":{"id":"/wiki/clients-42","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#client-implementations","content":"`booblik-native` on reposilite"},"5657":{"id":"/wiki/clients-43","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#client-implementations","content":"producer, consumer"},"5658":{"id":"/wiki/clients-44","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#client-implementations","content":"none"},"5659":{"id":"/wiki/clients-45","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#kotlinnative-multiplatform-target","content":"Unlike the other clients, the Kotlin/Native client is not a reimplementation but a target of the shared `booblik-protocol` (`README.md:5-8`). It shares the codec, the IDs, and the partitioner with the JVM client, allowing it to compile for both `linuxX64` and `macosArm64` from a single source (`README.md:22-23`)."},"5660":{"id":"/wiki/clients-46","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#kotlinnative-multiplatform-target","content":"More: Kotlin/Native Multiplatform Target"},"5661":{"id":"/wiki/clients-47","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#batching-and-throughput","content":"Performance is heavily dependent on batching; sending one record per request is considered the most expensive mistake possible (`README.md:22-23`). The broker's measurements show that batches of a hundred can achieve 4,335,482 records/s, compared to only 80,592 records/s when sent one at a time (`README.md:23`)."},"5662":{"id":"/wiki/clients-48","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#producer-and-consumer-mechanics","content":"In the .NET implementation, a `Producer` owns its `Connection` and acts as the sole writer to the socket; using the same `Connection` directly while a `Producer` is active can lead to mismatched responses (`README.md:44-45`). For reading, the .NET client uses `IAsyncEnumerable` to provide back-pressure, ensuring the next fetch does not occur until the current loop body is finished (`README.md:81-82`)."},"5663":{"id":"/wiki/clients-49","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#producer-and-consumer-mechanics","content":"More: Producer and Consumer Mechanics"},"5664":{"id":"/wiki/clients-50","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Checksum verification requires handling language-specific \"traps\" regarding integer types and hardware instructions (`README.md:37-40`)."},"5665":{"id":"/wiki/clients-51","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Language"},"5666":{"id":"/wiki/clients-52","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Implementation Detail"},"5667":{"id":"/wiki/clients-53","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Go"},"5668":{"id":"/wiki/clients-54","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Uses standard library with hardware instruction (`README.md:42-43`)"},"5669":{"id":"/wiki/clients-55","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Python"},"5670":{"id":"/wiki/clients-56","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Requires reading the stored sum as unsigned (`README.md:51`)"},"5671":{"id":"/wiki/clients-57","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"JavaScript"},"5672":{"id":"/wiki/clients-58","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Requires `>>> 0` because bitwise operators produce signed 32-bit results (`README.md:51`)"},"5673":{"id":"/wiki/clients-59","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Java"},"5674":{"id":"/wiki/clients-60","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Requires a cast from `CRC32C.getValue()`'s `long` (`README.md:52`)"},"5675":{"id":"/wiki/clients-61","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"C#"},"5676":{"id":"/wiki/clients-62","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Uses `unchecked` because FNV-1a arithmetic must wrap at 32 bits (`README.md:65-66`)"},"5677":{"id":"/wiki/clients-63","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Kotlin/Native"},"5678":{"id":"/wiki/clients-64","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"Uses a reflected polynomial in a 256-entry table (`README.md:77-78`)"},"5679":{"id":"/wiki/clients-65","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#crc-32c-checksum-implementation","content":"More: CRC-32C Checksum Implementation"},"5680":{"id":"/wiki/clients-66","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#conformance-and-gatekeeping","content":"Each client directory must contain `gate.sh` for ecosystem-specific checks and `conformance-client.sh` to execute the client under test (`README.md:79-80`). The `conformance-client.sh` script for .NET builds the assembly only if the source files are newer than the existing DLL (`conformance-client.sh:13-14`)."},"5681":{"id":"/wiki/clients-67","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"File"},"5682":{"id":"/wiki/clients-68","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"Lines"},"5683":{"id":"/wiki/clients-69","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"What is there"},"5684":{"id":"/wiki/clients-70","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`clients/README.md`"},"5685":{"id":"/wiki/clients-71","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`13-19`"},"5686":{"id":"/wiki/clients-72","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"Table of client coordinates, roles, and dependencies"},"5687":{"id":"/wiki/clients-73","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`…/dotnet/README.md`"},"5688":{"id":"/wiki/clients-74","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`20-42`"},"5689":{"id":"/wiki/clients-75","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"Documentation on batching and the `Producer` class"},"5690":{"id":"/wiki/clients-76","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`…/dotnet/gate.sh`"},"5691":{"id":"/wiki/clients-77","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`22-23`"},"5692":{"id":"/wiki/clients-78","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"Command to run `dotnet test`"},"5693":{"id":"/wiki/clients-79","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`…/kotlin-native/README.md`"},"5694":{"id":"/wiki/clients-80","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`28-31`"},"5695":{"id":"/wiki/clients-81","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"Explanation of the synchronous nature of the Native client"},"5696":{"id":"/wiki/clients-82","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`…/kotlin-native/conformance-client.sh`"},"5697":{"id":"/wiki/clients-83","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"`18-31`"},"5698":{"id":"/wiki/clients-84","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#key-files","content":"Logic to select the correct `TARGET` and `LINK_TASK` based on the host OS"},"5699":{"id":"/wiki/clients-85","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"`AckPolicy.None` in .NET results in nothing being returned at all, as no offset exists until the writer reaches the batch (`README.md:51-52`)."},"5700":{"id":"/wiki/clients-86","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `key` is never actually sent to the broker; the client calculates the partition and sends the number instead (`README.md:55-56`)."},"5701":{"id":"/wiki/clients-87","page_id":"/wiki/clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"`PartitionFor(null)` in both .NET and Kotlin/Native advances a round-robin counter, meaning the first call and the subsequent send constitute two turns of the counter (`README.md:62` and `README.md:95`)."},"5702":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","type":"page","content":"Kotlin/Native Multiplatform Target","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform"},"5703":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"5704":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#the-shared-protocol-and-booblik-protocol","content":"The shared protocol and booblik-protocol"},"5705":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#blocking-posix-sockets-and-the-absence-of-dispatchersio","content":"Blocking POSIX sockets and the absence of Dispatchers.IO"},"5706":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#the-producer-accumulator-and-newsinglethreadcontext","content":"The Producer accumulator and newSingleThreadContext"},"5707":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#crc32c-checksum-and-the-reflected-polynomial-trap","content":"CRC32C checksum and the reflected polynomial trap"},"5708":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#the-gatesh-and-conformance-clientsh-validation","content":"The gate.sh and conformance-client.sh validation"},"5709":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"Key files"},"5710":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"5711":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"5712":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#the-shared-protocol-and-booblik-protocol","content":"The Kotlin/Native client is not a reimplementation of the protocol but a target of the shared protocol. The codec, the IDs, and the partitioner are all sourced from `:booblik-protocol`, which compiles for the JVM and both native targets from a single source (`build.gradle.kts:37-38`). This ensures that the native client is a true target of the shared protocol rather than a fifth implementation."},"5713":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#blocking-posix-sockets-and-the-absence-of-dispatchersio","content":"The client uses blocking POSIX sockets and does not utilize coroutines for the core connection logic. Because `Dispatchers.IO` is `internal` in the Kotlin/Native runtime (`build.gradle.kts:18-19`), there is no standard IO pool to offload blocking socket calls onto. Consequently, the client remains synchronous, and the `Sequence` used in the consumer is chosen specifically because there is nothing to suspend on when the socket is a blocking POSIX one (`README.md:54-56`)."},"5714":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#the-producer-accumulator-and-newsinglethreadcontext","content":"While the `Connection` remains blocking, the `Producer` acts as an accumulator. Because the standard IO dispatcher is unavailable, the concurrency model requires a dedicated thread; specifically, `newSingleThreadContext` is used so that the producer owns a thread where both the loop and the socket reside (`build.gradle.kts:20-22`)."},"5715":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#crc32c-checksum-and-the-reflected-polynomial-trap","content":"Verifying a record's checksum is the responsibility of the client. This is implemented via `expect`/`actual` to allow the JVM to use its intrinsic `CRC32C` while Kotlin/Native uses a 256-entry table (`README.md:72-78`). A significant edge case occurred during development where a hand-derived negative literal `-0x7D644AC8` was used, which actually represents `0x829BB538`, resulting in a stable but incorrect sum (`README.md:82-84`)."},"5716":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#the-gatesh-and-conformance-clientsh-validation","content":"Validation is performed through two distinct scripts. `gate.sh` is used for local checks, selecting a target that the host can actually execute, such as `linuxX64` or `macosArm64` (`README.md:100-104`). The `conformance-client.sh` script handles the heavy lifting of building the binary if necessary and then executing it against a real broker (`conformance-client.sh:37-38`)."},"5717":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"File"},"5718":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"Lines"},"5719":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"What is there"},"5720":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"`booblik-native/build.gradle.kts`"},"5721":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"`37-38`"},"5722":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"Declaration of the shared `booblik-protocol` dependency"},"5723":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"`booblik-native/build.gradle.kts`"},"5724":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"`18-19`"},"5725":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"Note on the `internal` status of `Dispatchers.IO`"},"5726":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"`…/kotlin-native/README.md`"},"5727":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"`54-56`"},"5728":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"Explanation of why `Sequence` is used instead of `Flow`"},"5729":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"`…/kotlin-native/conformance-client.sh`"},"5730":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"`37-38`"},"5731":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#key-files","content":"Logic for building and executing the binary"},"5732":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"`produce` returns `null` when `AckPolicy.NONE` is used, because no offset exists until the writer reaches the batch (`README.md:89-90`)."},"5733":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"`partitionFor(null)` is not a static lookup but advances a round-robin counter (`README.md:95`)."},"5734":{"id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/kotlin-native-multiplatform#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `position` in a consumer is managed by the client and must be persisted *after* records are dealt with to ensure restart reliability (`README.md:62-63`)."},"5735":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"page","content":"Producer and Consumer Mechanics","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer"},"5736":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"5737":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#the-producer-and-the-connection-ownership","content":"The Producer and the Connection Ownership"},"5738":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"Batching Strategies: Manual vs Producer"},"5739":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#ackpolicynone-and-the-unknown-offset","content":"AckPolicy.None and the Unknown Offset"},"5740":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#consumer-position-and-iasyncenumerable-back-pressure","content":"Consumer Position and IAsyncEnumerable Back-pressure"},"5741":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#record-fragmentation-and-maxbytes","content":"Record Fragmentation and MaxBytes"},"5742":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#the-claims-log-and-worker-coordination","content":"The Claims Log and Worker Coordination"},"5743":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#the-write-actor-and-group-commit","content":"The Write Actor and Group Commit"},"5744":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"Key files"},"5745":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"5746":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"5747":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#the-producer-and-the-connection-ownership","content":"A `Producer` maintains exclusive ownership of its `Connection` to ensure that responses are matched correctly to requests in the order they were sent (`README.md:44-45`). Because a second writer on the same socket would take someone else's answer, the `Producer` is the sole writer to that socket (`README.md:44-45`). It is critical to use `DisposeAsync` to flush queued records; failing to do so results in silent data loss during shutdown (`README.md:46-47`)."},"5748":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"Performance is heavily dependent on batching, as single-record requests are significantly less efficient than batches of a hundred (`README.md:22-23`)."},"5749":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"Strategy"},"5750":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"Method"},"5751":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"Mechanism"},"5752":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"**Manual**"},"5753":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"`ProduceAsync`"},"5754":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"The user provides records already grouped together; they land contiguously from `result.Value.BaseOffset` (`README.md:29-30`)."},"5755":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"**Automatic**"},"5756":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"`Producer`"},"5757":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#batching-strategies-manual-vs-producer","content":"Uses a `Linger` configuration (e.g., 5ms) to collect records arriving one at a time into a batch (`README.md:36-42`)."},"5758":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#ackpolicynone-and-the-unknown-offset","content":"When using `AckPolicy.None`, the broker does not return an empty response, but rather nothing at all, because no offset exists until the writer reaches the batch (`README.md:51-52`). In this mode:"},"5759":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#ackpolicynone-and-the-unknown-offset","content":"`ProduceAsync` returns `null` (`README.md:52`)."},"5760":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#ackpolicynone-and-the-unknown-offset","content":"A `Producer` completes with `Producer.OffsetUnknown` (`README.md:52`)."},"5761":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#ackpolicynone-and-the-unknown-offset","content":"The broker may drop an accepted record silently (`README.md:53-54`)."},"5762":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#consumer-position-and-iasyncenumerable-back-pressure","content":"Reading is implemented via `IAsyncEnumerable`, which provides back-pressure by construction: the next fetch does not occur until the current loop body is finished (`README.md:81-82`). The `Position` property represents the number to be persisted; it is the responsibility of the caller to persist this *after* records are dealt with to ensure a restart re-delivers rather than skips data (`README.md:89-90`)."},"5763":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#record-fragmentation-and-maxbytes","content":"A consumer may encounter a stall if a record is larger than `MaxBytes`. In such cases, the record is never delivered whole, and a `RecordExceedsMaxBytesException` is thrown (`README.md:100`). Additionally, a response can stop inside a record if `MaxBytes` cuts on a byte boundary; the fragment is dropped, and the `Position` stops before it, requiring the next poll to request that record from its start (`README.md:102-103`)."},"5764":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#the-claims-log-and-worker-coordination","content":"In a task queue built on top of the log, the order of a specific partition acts as the arbiter for task acquisition (`Main.kt:39-40`). Workers write claims into a `claims` topic, and the first claim on a free task wins (`Main.kt:39-40`). This process involves a round-trip cost: a worker must write the claim and then read the log until the claim comes back to settle the task (`Main.kt:187-188`)."},"5765":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#the-write-actor-and-group-commit","content":"High-throughput durable writes are achieved through group commits, where a single barrier (operation) can service multiple producers (`benchmarking.md:263-264`). While a single producer is limited by the frequency of barriers (approx. 250/s), 64 producers can achieve over 7,500 durable writes per second by sharing the same disk barrier (`benchmarking.md:270-273`)."},"5766":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"File"},"5767":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"Lines"},"5768":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"What is there"},"5769":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/dotnet/README.md`"},"5770":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-34","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"`14-18`"},"5771":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-35","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"Usage example for `Connection` and `TopicAsync`"},"5772":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-36","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"5773":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"`157-161`"},"5774":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-38","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"Producer initialization and scope setup"},"5775":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-39","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"`docs/benchmarking.md`"},"5776":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-40","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"`243-245`"},"5777":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-41","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#key-files","content":"Performance data for `WRITTEN` vs `NONE` policies"},"5778":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-42","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`AckPolicy.None` does not return an empty response; it returns nothing, making it impossible to know if a record was accepted (`README.md:51`)."},"5779":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-43","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `key` is not sent to the broker; the client uses the key to pick a partition and sends only the partition number (`README.md:55-56`)."},"5780":{"id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer-44","page_id":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/producer-and-consumer#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`BrokerException` is a result of a declined request (framing was intact), not a connection outage (`README.md:59`)."},"5781":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"The `Conn` Lifecycle and Concurrency","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle"},"5782":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"5783":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#the-conn-thread-safety-model","content":"The `Conn` Thread-Safety Model"},"5784":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#the-booblikconnection-pipelining-mechanism","content":"The `BooblikConnection` Pipelining Mechanism"},"5785":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"Correlation ID Matching and `Pending` Requests"},"5786":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#failure-propagation-and-connectionclosedexception","content":"Failure Propagation and `ConnectionClosedException`"},"5787":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#error-handling-and-protocol-integrity","content":"Error Handling and Protocol Integrity"},"5788":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Key files"},"5789":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"5790":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"5791":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#the-conn-thread-safety-model","content":"The low-level `Conn` implementation in Go is explicitly not safe for concurrent use; it requires one connection per goroutine because requests and responses are matched by a correlation ID in the order they were sent, and sharing a `Conn` would cause goroutines to read each other's answers (`booblik.go:75-77`). In the Kotlin implementation, while the `BooblikConnection` is designed for concurrent access, it maintains this strict ordering by using a single writer coroutine to prevent interleaved writes (`BooblikConnection.kt:40-46`)."},"5792":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#the-booblikconnection-pipelining-mechanism","content":"To allow multiple callers to use a single connection without interleaving bytes, `BooblikConnection` uses a `Channel` to queue `Outgoing` messages (`BooblikConnection.kt:59`). A single writer coroutine drains this channel, ensuring that the order in which requests are enqueued is the exact order in which they are written to the `SocketChannel` (`BooblikConnection.kt:74-83`)."},"5793":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"Asynchronous responses are matched back to their original callers using a `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` of `Pending` objects (`BooblikConnection.kt:60`). The `Pending` sealed class hierarchy manages different response types:"},"5794":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"Class"},"5795":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"Purpose"},"5796":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"`Produce`"},"5797":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"Completes a `CompletableDeferred<ProduceResult>`"},"5798":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"`Metadata`"},"5799":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"Completes a `CompletableDeferred<MetadataResult>`"},"5800":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"`Fetch`"},"5801":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"Completes a `CompletableDeferred<FetchResult>`"},"5802":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#correlation-id-matching-and-pending-requests","content":"The `reader` coroutine polls the head of this queue and uses `checkOrder` to verify that the `correlationId` in the response matches the expected ID (`BooblikConnection.kt:192-195`)."},"5803":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#failure-propagation-and-connectionclosedexception","content":"When a connection failure occurs, the `fail` method is called to ensure no caller is left waiting forever (`BooblikConnection.kt:162-168`). This method iterates through all remaining `Pending` requests in the queue and calls `fail(ConnectionClosedException(cause))` on each one (`BooblikConnection.kt:166-167`)."},"5804":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#error-handling-and-protocol-integrity","content":"The client enforces protocol integrity by validating that the broker's response matches the request's `correlationId` (`BooblikConnection.kt:194`). If a response carries a different ID, it is treated as a critical error because it implies the broker is reordering responses, which would lead to delivering the wrong data to the wrong caller (`ErrorCodeTest.kt:19-21`). Additionally, the client must handle `ErrorCode` responses, such as `UNSUPPORTED_VERSION`, which are returned as part of the response frame (`ErrorCodeTest.kt:39-40`)."},"5805":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"File"},"5806":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Lines"},"5807":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"What is there"},"5808":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5809":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`78-81`"},"5810":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Definition of the non-thread-safe `Conn` struct"},"5811":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`…/client/BooblikConnection.kt`"},"5812":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`50-61`"},"5813":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"The `BooblikConnection` class and its internal queueing mechanism"},"5814":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`…/client/BooblikConnection.kt`"},"5815":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`184-241`"},"5816":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-34","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#key-files","content":"The `Pending` sealed class hierarchy for request matching"},"5817":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-35","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Strict Ordering Requirement**: A `BooblikConnection` is a pipelined connection where the broker is expected to answer in strict FIFO order; if the broker reorders responses, the client will throw an error rather than silently delivering the wrong data (`BooblikConnection.kt:34-38`)."},"5818":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-36","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Health Check Semantics**: A health check using `Metadata` is preferred over a simple TCP connect because a TCP handshake can succeed even if the broker process is hung and unable to process requests (`Health.kt:14-18`)."},"5819":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-conn-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Correlation ID Persistence**: Even when the broker returns an error like `UNSUPPORTED_VERSION`, it is required to echo the `correlationId` so the client can identify which request failed (`ErrorCodeTest.kt:39-40`)."},"5820":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","type":"page","content":"The `Topic` and Partitioning Logic","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and"},"5821":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"5822":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#topic","content":"Topic"},"5823":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#partitionfor","content":"PartitionFor"},"5824":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#metadata","content":"Metadata"},"5825":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#partitionregistry","content":"PartitionRegistry"},"5826":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#metadatatest","content":"MetadataTest"},"5827":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"Key files"},"5828":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"5829":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"5830":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#topic","content":"The abstraction of a named stream of records and its relationship to the broker's metadata. A `Topic` represents a logical grouping of partitions, and its structure is determined by the broker's current state. In the Kotlin client, a `TopicHandle` is obtained from a `Producer` and contains the `TopicName` and a list of available `PartitionId`s (`Publishing.kt:19-22`)."},"5831":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#partitionfor","content":"The mechanics of mapping a record key to a specific `PartitionId` using round-robin or hashing. The selection logic depends on whether a key is provided:"},"5832":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#partitionfor","content":"**Unkeyed records**: Uses a round-robin strategy where a counter is incremented to ensure an even spread across partitions (`Publishing.kt:40-42`)."},"5833":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#partitionfor","content":"**Keyed records**: Uses a `Partitioner` (defaulting to `Fnv1a`) to map the key to a partition (`booblik-client/src/main/kotlin/ru/workinprogress/booblik/net/client/Publishing.kt:23, 44`)."},"5834":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#metadata","content":"The protocol for discovering existing topics and the error behavior when requesting unknown topics. The client can request metadata for all topics or a specific subset (`BooblikClient.kt:70`). If a requested topic does not exist on the broker, the request fails with an `UNKNOWN_TOPIC_OR_PARTITION` error rather than simply omitting the topic from the response (`BooblikServer.kt:75-77`)."},"5835":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#partitionregistry","content":"The server-side management of topic-partition mappings and the ordering of responses. The `PartitionRegistry` holds a map of `Key` (topic and partition) to `PartitionHandle` (`BooblikServer.kt:79-81`). When describing all partitions, the registry ensures the output is ordered by topic name and then by partition ID to ensure deterministic response ordering (`BooblikServer.kt:94-97`)."},"5836":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#metadatatest","content":"Verification of topic existence, partition counts, and the distinction between empty and non-existent topics. The tests verify that:"},"5837":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#metadatatest","content":"An empty metadata request describes every topic the broker has (`MetadataTest.kt:30-37`)."},"5838":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#metadatatest","content":"Naming specific topics narrows the response to only those topics (`MetadataTest.kt:50-55`)."},"5839":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#metadatatest","content":"An unknown topic results in an error rather than an empty list, preventing ambiguity between a non-existent topic and an empty one (`MetadataTest.kt:58-63`)."},"5840":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"File"},"5841":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"Lines"},"5842":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"What is there"},"5843":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"`…/client/Publishing.kt`"},"5844":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"`19-46`"},"5845":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"`TopicHandle` class and its partitioning logic"},"5846":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"`…/net/BooblikServer.kt`"},"5847":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"`79-107`"},"5848":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"`PartitionRegistry` and its key/ordering logic"},"5849":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"`…/net/MetadataTest.kt`"},"5850":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"`28-77`"},"5851":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#key-files","content":"Tests for metadata discovery and error handling"},"5852":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**Round-robin vs Random**: In `TopicHandle.partitionFor`, round-robin is used instead of random for unkeyed records because random distribution can visibly clump at small counts, which might be mistaken for a bug (`Publishing.kt:31-33`)."},"5853":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**Error vs Omission**: Requesting a non-existent topic via `metadata` returns an error code rather than an empty list to distinguish \"no such topic\" from \"topic exists but is empty\" (`BooblikServer.kt:75-77`)."},"5854":{"id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients/the-topic-and#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**Deterministic Ordering**: The `PartitionRegistry.describe` function explicitly sorts entries by topic and partition to ensure that identical requests result in identical response byte sequences (`BooblikServer.kt:94-97`)."},"5855":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"page","content":"Conn","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients/go"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn"},"5856":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"5857":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"5858":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#the-conn-lifecycle-and-dial","content":"The `Conn` lifecycle and `Dial`"},"5859":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#request-response-matching-via-correlation","content":"Request-Response matching via `correlation`"},"5860":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#context-driven-deadlines-and-withcontext","content":"Context-driven deadlines and `withContext`"},"5861":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#the-receive-mechanism-and-frame-validation","content":"The `receive` mechanism and frame validation"},"5862":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#error-handling-and-connection-stability","content":"Error handling and connection stability"},"5863":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#testing-acknone-and-response-timeouts","content":"Testing `AckNone` and response timeouts"},"5864":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"Key files"},"5865":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"5866":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"5867":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `Conn` type is the fundamental transport layer for the `booblik` Go client. It manages a single TCP connection to a broker and handles the low-level framing of the wire protocol. It is responsible for serializing request headers, managing correlation IDs to ensure request-response integrity, and enforcing protocol-level constraints like maximum frame sizes."},"5868":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#the-conn-lifecycle-and-dial","content":"A connection is established using the `Dial` function, which takes a `context.Context` to bound the initial connection attempt (`booblik.go:85-94`). Once established, the connection is not safe for concurrent use because requests and responses are matched by a strictly incrementing `correlation` ID (`booblik.go:75-77`). To ensure low latency for the small requests typical of this protocol, the client relies on `TCP_NODELAY` being enabled by default in Go (`booblik.go:91-93`)."},"5869":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#request-response-matching-via-correlation","content":"To ensure that a response is matched to the correct caller, the `Conn` maintains an internal `correlation` counter (`booblik.go:80`). Every time `send` is called, the `correlation` is incremented and included in the request header (`booblik.go:125-132`). During the `receive` phase, the client explicitly checks that the `correlation` ID returned by the broker matches the one expected for that specific request; if they do not match, the client returns an error to prevent one caller from receiving another's data (`booblik.go:162-164`)."},"5870":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#context-driven-deadlines-and-withcontext","content":"Since Go's standard socket operations do not natively support `context.Context` for interruption, `Conn` implements a `withContext` method to bridge this gap (`booblik.go:103-122`). This method works by moving the connection's deadline into the past to interrupt blocking operations if the context is cancelled or reaches its deadline (`booblik.go:104-114`). It returns an undo function that restores the deadline once the exchange is complete (`booblik.go:118-121`)."},"5871":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#the-receive-mechanism-and-frame-validation","content":"The `receive` method performs strict validation on incoming data to protect the client from malformed or malicious responses (`booblik.go:141-168`). The validation steps include:"},"5872":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#the-receive-mechanism-and-frame-validation","content":"Reading the 4-byte length prefix (`booblik.go:144-146`)."},"5873":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#the-receive-mechanism-and-frame-validation","content":"Ensuring the length is within the bounds of `responseHeaderBytes` and `maxFrameBytes` (`booblik.go:147-149`)."},"5874":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#the-receive-mechanism-and-frame-validation","content":"Verifying the `correlation` ID matches the expected value (`booblik.go:162-164`)."},"5875":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#the-receive-mechanism-and-frame-validation","content":"Checking that the response `code` is `CodeNone` (`booblik.go:165-167`)."},"5876":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#error-handling-and-connection-stability","content":"The client distinguishes between protocol errors and transport errors to maintain connection stability (`booblik_test.go:74-90`)."},"5877":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#error-handling-and-connection-stability","content":"**Usable Errors:** Errors like `UNKNOWN_TOPIC_OR_PARTITION` are considered protocol-level refusals; because the framing remains intact, the connection remains usable for subsequent requests (`booblik_test.go:76-88`)."},"5878":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#error-handling-and-connection-stability","content":"**Fatal Errors:** If a response frame length is out of range, the connection is effectively compromised and cannot be safely reused (`booblik.go:147-149`)."},"5879":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#testing-acknone-and-response-timeouts","content":"The client must handle the `AckNone` policy, where the broker does not send a response (`booblik.go:50-52`). Tests verify that when `AckNone` is used, the `Produce` call returns immediately with a `nil` result and no error, rather than waiting for a response that will never arrive (`booblik_test.go:57-72`)."},"5880":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"File"},"5881":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"Lines"},"5882":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"What is there"},"5883":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5884":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`78-81`"},"5885":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"Definition of the `Conn` struct and its fields."},"5886":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5887":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`124-139`"},"5888":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"The `send` method implementation."},"5889":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5890":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-34","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`141-169`"},"5891":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-35","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"The `receive` method implementation."},"5892":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-36","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik_test.go`"},"5893":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`22-47`"},"5894":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-38","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"Round-trip production testing."},"5895":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-39","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik_test.go`"},"5896":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-40","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"`52-72`"},"5897":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-41","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#key-files","content":"Testing for `AckNone` behavior."},"5898":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-42","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`Conn.send` increments the `correlation` ID internally, meaning the caller does not manage the sequence of IDs (`booblik.go:125`)."},"5899":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-43","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Using `AckNone` in a `Produce` call results in a `nil` `ProduceResult` because there is no response to parse (`booblik.go:262-263`)."},"5900":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn-44","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/conn","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/conn#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"A `Conn` is not thread-safe; sharing a single `Conn` between goroutines will cause them to read each other's responses due to the shared `correlation` state (`booblik.go:75-77`)."},"5901":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"page","content":"clients/go","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients/go"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go"},"5902":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"5903":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"5904":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#conn","content":"Conn"},"5905":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#produceresult","content":"ProduceResult"},"5906":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"AckPolicy"},"5907":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#topic","content":"Topic"},"5908":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"PartitionInfo"},"5909":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#the-fetch-mechanism","content":"The Fetch mechanism"},"5910":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#producer","content":"Producer"},"5911":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"Key files"},"5912":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go#behaviour-that-not-obvious","content":"Behaviour that not obvious"},"5913":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"5914":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `booblik` Go module is a client for the booblik message broker, providing the ability to produce, fetch, and retrieve metadata. It is designed to handle the complexities of an append-only log, including checksum validation, partition routing, and efficient batching for high-throughput writes."},"5915":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#conn","content":"The `Conn` type represents a single TCP connection to a broker and is not safe for concurrent use because requests and responses are matched by a correlation ID in the order they are sent (`booblik.go:78-81`). Users should use one `Conn` per goroutine or utilize a `Producer` which manages its own connection (`booblik.go:78-81`). The `Dial` function opens this connection using a `net.Dialer` (`booblik.go:85-87`)."},"5916":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#conn","content":"More: Conn"},"5917":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#produceresult","content":"A successful batch write returns a `ProduceResult` containing the offsets assigned to the batch (`booblik.go:70-71`). Specifically, it provides the `BaseOffset` and the `LogEndOffset` (`booblik.go:70-71`)."},"5918":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"The `AckPolicy` defines how long the broker waits before responding to a write request (`booblik.go:43-44`)."},"5919":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"Mode"},"5920":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"Value"},"5921":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"Description"},"5922":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"`AckNone`"},"5923":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"0"},"5924":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"No response is sent; the client receives no offset and the broker may drop the record (`booblik.go:50-51`)."},"5925":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"`AckWritten`"},"5926":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"1"},"5927":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"The broker responds once the record is in the log, before any durability barrier (`booblik.go:52`)."},"5928":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"`AckForced`"},"5929":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"2"},"5930":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#ackpolicy","content":"The broker responds after a `force()` operation, grouping all queued requests into one (`booblik.go:55`)."},"5931":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#topic","content":"The `Topic` struct manages partition information and routing for a specific topic name (`booblik.go:281-284`). It provides the `PartitionFor` method to determine which partition a record should be sent to (`booblik.go:318`). If a `nil` key is provided, it uses a round-robin counter to select a partition (`booblik.go:318-320`)."},"5932":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"`PartitionInfo` contains metadata retrieved via the `METADATA` API regarding the state of a partition (`booblik.go:59-67`)."},"5933":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"Field"},"5934":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"Type"},"5935":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"Description"},"5936":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-34","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"`Partition`"},"5937":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-35","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"`int32`"},"5938":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-36","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"The partition ID (`booblik.go:60`)"},"5939":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"`LogStartOffset`"},"5940":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-38","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"`int64`"},"5941":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-39","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"The offset where the live log begins after retention (`booblik.go:62-64`)"},"5942":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-40","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"`HighWatermark`"},"5943":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-41","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"`int64`"},"5944":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-42","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#partitioninfo","content":"The first offset that does not exist yet (`booblik.go:66`)"},"5945":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-43","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#the-fetch-mechanism","content":"Reading is performed via the `Records` method, which is a range-over-func iterator that runs on the caller's goroutine (`booblik.go:59-60`). The mechanism handles several edge cases:"},"5946":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-44","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#the-fetch-mechanism","content":"**Truncated Tails**: A response might be cut at a byte boundary by `MaxBytes`, resulting in a partial record where the client must stop at the last complete record (`booblik.go:95-96`)."},"5947":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-45","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#the-fetch-mechanism","content":"**MaxWait**: The broker can hold a request for up to `MaxWait` (default 5s) to allow more records to accumulate before responding (`booblik.go:92-93`)."},"5948":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-46","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#the-fetch-mechanism","content":"**High Watermark**: A consumer that has caught up will receive an empty response at the high watermark (`booblik.go:91-92`)."},"5949":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-47","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#the-fetch-mechanism","content":"More: The Fetch mechanism"},"5950":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-48","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#producer","content":"The `Producer` is an abstraction used when records arrive one at a time and need to be batched for performance (`booblik.go:40-41`). A `Producer` owns its own `Conn` and is the only writer to its socket to prevent interleaved responses (`booblik.go:50-52`). It is initialized via `NewProducer` and must be closed to flush queued records (`booblik.go:43-44`)."},"5951":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-49","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"File"},"5952":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-50","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"Lines"},"5953":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-51","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"What is there"},"5954":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-52","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5955":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-53","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`21-40`"},"5956":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-54","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"Constants for API versions and header byte sizes"},"5957":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-55","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5958":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-56","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`43-56`"},"5959":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-57","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`AckPolicy` enumeration and descriptions"},"5960":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-58","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5961":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-59","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`59-67`"},"5962":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-60","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`PartitionInfo` struct definition"},"5963":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-61","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5964":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-62","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`78-81`"},"5965":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-63","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`Conn` struct and concurrency warnings"},"5966":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-64","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5967":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-65","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`281-287`"},"5968":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-66","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`Topic` struct and round-robin field"},"5969":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-67","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`…/go/booblik.go`"},"5970":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-68","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`318-323`"},"5971":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-69","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#key-files","content":"`PartitionFor` implementation"},"5972":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-70","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#behaviour-that-not-obvious","content":"`Conn.send` increments the `correlation` ID for every request, and `receive` validates that the response's correlation ID matches the expected one to prevent one caller from receiving another's answer (`booblik.go:125, 162`)."},"5973":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-71","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#behaviour-that-not-obvious","content":"`Topic.PartitionFor` with a `nil` key advances an internal `roundRobin` counter, meaning that calling `PartitionFor` twice without sending a message will result in two different partition selections (`booblik.go:318-320`)."},"5974":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go-72","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go#behaviour-that-not-obvious","content":"`Produce` with `AckNone` returns `(nil, nil)` because no offset exists until the broker reaches the batch, meaning there is no response to return (`booblik.go:262-263`)."},"5975":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"page","content":"The Fetch mechanism","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients/go"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism"},"5976":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"5977":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"FetchRequest"},"5978":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetched","content":"Fetched"},"5979":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#truncation-and-maxbytes","content":"Truncation and MaxBytes"},"5980":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#corruptrecorderror","content":"CorruptRecordError"},"5981":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#recordexceedsmaxbyteserror","content":"RecordExceedsMaxBytesError"},"5982":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#consumer-position-and-lag","content":"Consumer Position and Lag"},"5983":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#records-iterator","content":"Records Iterator"},"5984":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"Key files"},"5985":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"5986":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"5987":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"The `FetchRequest` struct defines the parameters that govern how a consumer requests data from a partition `consumer.go:94-112`. The request includes the target `Topic`, `Partition`, and the starting `Offset`."},"5988":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"Parameter"},"5989":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"Type"},"5990":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"Description"},"5991":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"`Offset`"},"5992":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"`int64`"},"5993":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"The starting point for reading."},"5994":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"`MaxBytes`"},"5995":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"`int32`"},"5996":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"Bounds the response in bytes, not records."},"5997":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"`MaxWait`"},"5998":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"`time.Duration`"},"5999":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"How long the broker holds a request if no data is available."},"6000":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"`MinBytes`"},"6001":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"`int32`"},"6002":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"The minimum amount of data required before the broker responds."},"6003":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetchrequest","content":"A critical distinction exists between `MaxBytes` and the size of individual records: `MaxBytes` limits the total response size, which can cause a response to end in the middle of a record `consumer.go:100-101`."},"6004":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#fetched","content":"A successful response is encapsulated in the `Fetched` struct `consumer.go:115-129`. It contains the `HighWatermark`, which represents the first offset that does not yet exist in the log. The `Records` field is a slice of byte slices that point directly into the response frame without copying the data, meaning the caller must copy any data they wish to retain beyond the lifetime of the frame `consumer.go:118-120`."},"6005":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#truncation-and-maxbytes","content":"Because `MaxBytes` limits the response size in bytes, a response may end inside a record. This is indicated by the `Truncated` flag in the `Fetched` struct `consumer.go:123-125`. When this occurs, the `TruncatedRecordBytes` field provides the size of the record that was cut off, allowing the client to understand why the response was incomplete `consumer.go:127-128`."},"6006":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#corruptrecorderror","content":"The client is the only party capable of detecting data corruption on the read path because the broker uses zero-copy to stream segment bytes directly to the socket without inspecting them `consumer.go:41-43`. The client verifies each record using CRC-32C (Castagnoli) via the `Checksum` function `consumer.go:36`. If the computed checksum does not match the stored checksum, a `CorruptRecordError` is returned, which includes the `Offset`, the `Stored` checksum, and the `Computed` checksum `consumer.go:57-69`."},"6007":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#recordexceedsmaxbyteserror","content":"A permanent stall occurs if a single record is larger than the consumer's `MaxBytes` limit. In this scenario, the broker returns a truncated response, and the client identifies this as a `RecordExceedsMaxBytesError` `consumer.go:54-55`. This error carries the `Offset`, the `RecordBytes` required, and the current `MaxBytes` limit to inform the caller that they must increase their buffer to proceed `consumer.go:74-85`."},"6008":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#consumer-position-and-lag","content":"The `Consumer` manages the `position` (the offset of the next record to be read) and the `highWatermark` `consumer.go:243-250`. The position is only advanced past whole records during a `Poll` operation `consumer.go:347`. `Lag` is calculated as the difference between the `highWatermark` and the current `position`, representing how many records the consumer is behind the head of the log `consumer.go:306-310`."},"6009":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#records-iterator","content":"The `Records` method provides a range-over-func iterator that yields records one at a time `consumer.go:376-377`. This iterator runs on the caller's goroutine at the caller's pace, avoiding the issues of buffered channels such as leaking goroutines or silently advancing the position past unhandled records `consumer.go:366-371`."},"6010":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-34","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"File"},"6011":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-35","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6012":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-36","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6013":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`…/go/consumer.go`"},"6014":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-38","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`94-112`"},"6015":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-39","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`FetchRequest` struct definition"},"6016":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-40","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`…/go/consumer.go`"},"6017":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-41","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`115-129`"},"6018":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-42","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`Fetched` struct definition"},"6019":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-43","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`…/go/consumer.go`"},"6020":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-44","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`57-69`"},"6021":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-45","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`CorruptRecordError` struct definition"},"6022":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-46","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`…/go/consumer.go`"},"6023":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-47","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`74-85`"},"6024":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-48","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`RecordExceedsMaxBytesError` struct definition"},"6025":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-49","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`…/go/consumer.go`"},"6026":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-50","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`243-250`"},"6027":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-51","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#key-files","content":"`Consumer` struct definition"},"6028":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-52","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`Poll` only advances the `position` past whole records; if a response is truncated, the position remains at the start of the partial record to allow for a retry with a larger `MaxBytes` `consumer.go:347`."},"6029":{"id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism-53","page_id":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-go/the-fetch-mechanism#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `Records` iterator does not use a channel; it executes on the caller's goroutine to ensure that the `position` is not advanced until the caller has actually processed the records `consumer.go:371`."},"6030":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"page","content":"Connection","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients/dotnet/Booblik.Tests"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection"},"6031":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"6032":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6033":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#connectionconnectasync","content":"Connection.ConnectAsync"},"6034":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#ackpolicynone","content":"AckPolicy.None"},"6035":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#protocolexception-and-protocolerror","content":"ProtocolException and ProtocolError"},"6036":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#socketchannel-and-transferto","content":"SocketChannel and transferTo"},"6037":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#partial-frame-assembly","content":"Partial Frame Assembly"},"6038":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#broker-refusals","content":"Broker Refusals"},"6039":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6040":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#behaviour-that-すれthought","content":"Behaviour that すれthought"},"6041":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6042":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `Connection` component manages the lifecycle and communication between a client and a Booblik broker. It is responsible for establishing asynchronous connections, handling the framing of requests and responses, managing data transfer efficiency via zero-copy mechanisms, and ensuring that protocol errors are correctly distinguished from connection-level failures."},"6043":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#connectionconnectasync","content":"The asynchronous establishment of a connection to a broker is the entry point for client interaction, as seen in `ConnectionTests.cs:22` and `connection.test.js:19`. This process initializes the underlying transport and prepares the client to send framed requests."},"6044":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#ackpolicynone","content":"The behavior of asynchronous production when no response is expected from the broker is governed by `AckPolicy.None`. When this policy is used, the client does not wait for a response from the broker, which is verified by ensuring the `produce` task returns `null` or completes without waiting for a broker acknowledgement (`connection.test.js:46` and `ServerTest.kt:65`)."},"6045":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#protocolexception-and-protocolerror","content":"Handling of malformed frames and truncated responses during decoding is critical for stability. A response that is cut short by a broker restart is treated as a `ProtocolException` rather than a standard decoding error (`ConnectionTests.cs:102`), and such malformed data must trigger a `ProtocolError` to prevent the caller from receiving invalid state (`connection.test.js:88`)."},"6046":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#socketchannel-and-transferto","content":"The mechanics of zero-copy data transfer rely on the `transferTo` method, which requires the underlying object to be a real `SocketChannel` to utilize the JDK's direct `sendfile` path (`SendfileTest.kt:72`). If the connection is wrapped in a decorator (like a metrics or TLS layer), the direct path is lost, even though the resulting bytes remain identical (`SendfileTest.kt:101`)."},"6047":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#partial-frame-assembly","content":"The system is designed to handle fragmented network traffic through robust assembly logic:"},"6048":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#partial-frame-assembly","content":"**Split Packets:** A frame split across two packets is correctly assembled once the remaining bytes arrive (`PartialFrameTest.kt:40`)."},"6049":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#partial-frame-assembly","content":"**Byte-at-a-time:** Data delivered one byte at a time is still successfully assembled into a single request (`PartialFrameTest.kt:65`)."},"6050":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#partial-frame-assembly","content":"**Absurd Lengths:** If a frame specifies an absurdly large length, the broker must drop the connection rather than attempting to allocate massive amounts of memory (`PartialFrameTest.kt:112`)."},"6051":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#broker-refusals","content":"The distinction between protocol errors that close a connection and errors that allow connection reuse is vital for reliability. A refusal (such as `UnknownTopicOrPartition`) is a protocol-level error that does not close the connection if the framing remains intact (`ConnectionTests.cs:53`), whereas a frame length out of range is a framing error that must close the connection (`ConnectionTests.cs:61`)."},"6052":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"File"},"6053":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6054":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6055":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`…/Booblik.Tests/ConnectionTests.cs`"},"6056":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`16-31`"},"6057":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"Tests for byte-for-byte record arrival and offset verification"},"6058":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`…/net/PartialFrameTest.kt`"},"6059":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`40-57`"},"6060":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"Tests for frame splitting and delayed packet arrival"},"6061":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`…/net/SendfileTest.kt`"},"6062":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`69-78`"},"6063":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"Verification that `transferTo` maintains the `SocketChannel` type"},"6064":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`…/test/connection.test.js`"},"6065":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-34","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`28-38`"},"6066":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-35","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"Node.js implementation of byte-for-byte record testing"},"6067":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-36","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`…/harness/scenarios.py`"},"6068":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"`81-94`"},"6069":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-38","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#key-files","content":"Python conformance check for byte-for-byte payload integrity"},"6070":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-39","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#behaviour-that-すれthought","content":"`AckPolicy.None` results in the client not receiving an offset, as the broker sends nothing back (`connection.test.js:48`)."},"6071":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-40","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#behaviour-that-すれthought","content":"A `ProtocolException` is specifically used to signal that a response was truncated, distinguishing it from a standard `RangeError` during decoding (`ConnectionTests.cs:102`)."},"6072":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection-41","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests/connection#behaviour-that-すれthought","content":"The `transferTo` path is highly sensitive to object wrapping; wrapping a `SocketChannel` in a decorator can silently disable ze