oblik.Tests-53","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests#key-files","content":"`17-51`"},"6176":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests-54","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests#key-files","content":"Linger window and record loss regression test"},"6177":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests-55","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests#behaviour-that-does-not-surprise","content":"`Producer.SendAsync` returns `Producer.OffsetUnknown` when `AckPolicy.None` is used, rather than waiting for a broker response `ProducerTests.cs:132-134`."},"6178":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests-56","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests#behaviour-that-does-not-surprise","content":"`Consumer.PollAsync` will throw a `RecordExceedsMaxBytesException` if a single record is larger than the configured `MaxBytes`, preventing an infinite retry loop `ConsumerTests.cs:145-150`."},"6179":{"id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests-57","page_id":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-dotnet-Booblik.Tests#behaviour-that-does-not-surprise","content":"`FakeBroker` uses a `lock` on `_gate` to ensure thread-safe access to the `_produced` dictionary and `_nextOffset` `FakeBroker.cs:25-28`."},"6180":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"page","content":"clients/python","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients/python"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python"},"6181":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6182":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-connection-lifecycle","content":"The `Connection` lifecycle"},"6183":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-producer-and-topic-interaction","content":"The `Producer` and `Topic` interaction"},"6184":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-consumer-and-fetched-data-stream","content":"The `Consumer` and `Fetched` data stream"},"6185":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-crc32c-checksum-mechanism","content":"The `crc32c` checksum mechanism"},"6186":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-partition_for-hashing-strategy","content":"The `partition_for` hashing strategy"},"6187":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python#error-handling-and-brokererror-codes","content":"Error handling and `BrokerError` codes"},"6188":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6189":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-python#behaviour-that-does-not-surprise","content":"Behaviour that does not surprise"},"6190":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6191":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-connection-lifecycle","content":"The `Connection` class manages a synchronous, blocking TCP socket, ensuring that requests and responses are matched via a unique correlation ID (`connection.py:34`). It handles the low-level exchange of bytes through `_exchange` (`connection.py:58`), which reads a length-prefixed frame and then uses `wire.read_header` to validate the response's correlation ID and error code (`connection.py:60`)."},"6192":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-producer-and-topic-interaction","content":"Users can publish data using `Producer.send` or via a `Topic` handle, which simplifies routing by managing partition selection (`connection.py:174`). When a `None` key is provided, the `Topic.partition_for` method uses a round-robin strategy by incrementing an internal counter (`connection.py:191-193`). For non-null keys, the client ensures deterministic routing by hashing the key to a specific partition (`connection.py:194`)."},"6193":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-producer-and-topic-interaction","content":"More: The `Producer` and `Topic` interaction"},"6194":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-consumer-and-fetched-data-stream","content":"The `Consumer` provides a high-level interface to read records from a specific partition starting from a given offset (`connection.py:151`). Data is returned as a `Fetched` object, which includes a `truncated` flag to indicate if a response was cut short because a record exceeded `max_bytes` (`wire.py:77-82`). Every record in the stream is verified using `crc32c` to ensure data integrity (`wire.py:163-164`)."},"6195":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-crc32c-checksum-mechanism","content":"The client implements the Castagnoli CRC-32C polynomial (`0x1EDC6F41`) using a pre-computed lookup table for efficiency (`crc32c.py:31-48`). The implementation uses a reflected polynomial (`0x82F63B78`) and bit-shifting logic to process data without external dependencies (`crc32c.py:41`)."},"6196":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#the-partition_for-hashing-strategy","content":"Partitioning is achieved through the `fnv1a32` function, which implements the 32-bit FNV-1a hash algorithm (`partition.py:19`). This hash is then \"folded\" into the available partition range using a modulo operation (`partition.py:44`)."},"6197":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#error-handling-and-brokererror-codes","content":"Errors are categorized into protocol-level failures and broker-side refusals. Broker refusals are represented by the `Code` enumeration (`errors.py:6-12`):"},"6198":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#error-handling-and-brokererror-codes","content":"Code"},"6199":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#error-handling-and-brokererror-codes","content":"Value"},"6200":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#error-handling-and-brokererror-codes","content":"Description"},"6201":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#error-handling-and-brokererror-codes","content":"NONE"},"6202":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#error-handling-and-brokererror-codes","content":"0"},"6203":{"id":"/wiki/clients-python-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-python#error-handling-and-brokererror-codes","content":