cation-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-crc32c-function-and-the-castagnoli-polynomial","content":"The `crc32c` function and the Castagnoli polynomial"},"6342":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-table-and-the-reflected-polynomial","content":"The `TABLE` and the reflected polynomial"},"6343":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"The `RECORD_HEADER` and the `checksum` method"},"6344":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-fetch-response-and-corruptrecordexception","content":"The `fetch` response and `CorruptRecordException`"},"6345":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-recovery-process-and-torn-records","content":"The `recovery` process and torn records"},"6346":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-conformancevectorscrc32ctsv-and-gatesh-tests","content":"The `conformance/vectors/crc32c.tsv` and `gate.sh` tests"},"6347":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6348":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"6349":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6350":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-crc32c-function-and-the-castagnoli-polynomial","content":"The implementation uses the Castagnoli polynomial `0x1EDC6F41`, which is distinct from the standard CRC-32 polynomial `0x04C11DB7` used by `zlib.crc32` (`crc32c.js:3-6`). Using the wrong polynomial is a \"silent way to get this wrong,\" as it results in a stable but entirely incorrect sum that causes clients to reject every record (`crc32c.js:24-25`)."},"6351":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-table-and-the-reflected-polynomial","content":"To optimize performance, the implementation uses a precomputed 256-entry lookup table (`crc32c.js:42`). This table is built using the **reflected** form of the polynomial, `0x82F63B78`, because the algorithm uses right-shifting operations (`crc32c.js:15-16`). This precomputation ensures that rebuilding the table per record would be prohibitively expensive (`crc32c.py:47-48`)."},"6352":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"Data is framed in segments using a specific header format. The structure of a record is defined as follows:"},"6353":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"Field"},"6354":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"Size"},"6355":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"Description"},"6356":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"Length"},"6357":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"`Int.SIZE_BYTES`"},"6358":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"The size of the payload"},"6359":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"CRC"},"6360":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"`Int.SIZE_BYTES`"},"6361":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"The CRC32C checksum of the payload"},"6362":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-record_header-and-the-checksum-method","content":"The broker computes this sum using the `checksum` method, which updates a `java.util.zip.CRC32C` instance with the payload bytes (`SegmentWriter.kt:93-95`)."},"6363":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-fetch-response-and-corruptrecordexception","content":"Because the broker uses zero-copy mechanisms, it streams segment bytes to the socket without inspecting them (`ResponseReader.kt:36-37`). Consequently, the client is the only party capable of detecting corruption on the read path. If a record's bytes do not match the checksum provided in the frame, the client throws a `CorruptRecordException` (`ResponseReader.kt:39-41`)."},"6364":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-recovery-process-and-torn-records","content":"Checksums are critical during the log recovery process to handle \"torn records\"—records that were only partially written when a process crashed (`SegmentWriter.kt:47-48`). Without a checksum, recovery might mistake a partial body for valid data; with it, recovery can identify and stop at the first record where the bytes do not match the header (`SegmentWriter.kt:72-73`)."},"6365":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-conformancevectorscrc32ctsv-and-gatesh-tests","content":"The implementation is pinned by conformance vectors to ensure mathematical correctness (`crc32c.js:22`). Validation is performed through two layers:"},"6366":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-conformancevectorscrc32ctsv-and-gatesh-tests","content":"**Encoding/Decoding Consistency:** Tests run against a fake broker that decodes what the client encodes to catch encoding mistakes (`README.md:120-121`)."},"6367":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#the-conformancevectorscrc32ctsv-and-gatesh-tests","content":"**Conformance:** Running against a real broker ensures the client is correct in a real-world environment (`README.md:118`)."},"6368":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"File"},"6369":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6370":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6371":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"`…/src/crc32c.js`"},"6372":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"`24-25`"},"6373":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"The Castagnoli polynomial constant"},"6374":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"`…/storage/SegmentWriter.kt`"},"6375":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-34","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"`88-96`"},"6376":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-35","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"The `checksum` method for computing payload sums"},"6377":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-36","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"`…/client/ResponseReader.kt`"},"6378":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"`39-41`"},"6379":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-38","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"The `CorruptRecordException` definition"},"6380":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-39","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"`…/booblik/crc32c.py`"},"6381":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-40","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"`31`"},"6382":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-41","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#key-files","content":"The reflected polynomial constant"},"6383":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-42","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Bitwise Arithmetic:** In JavaScript, the `>>> 0` operator is required at the end of the checksum calculation because bitwise operators produce signed 32-bit results, and a negative sum will fail to match the broker's unsigned sum (`crc32c.js:17-19`)."},"6384":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification-43","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/crc-checksum-verification#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Zero-Copy Verification:** Because the broker uses zero-copy, it cannot verify the data it sends; verification is strictly a client-side responsibility (`SegmentWriter.kt:78-80`)."},"6385":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","type":"page","content":"FNV-1a Partitioning","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients/node"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning"},"6386":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6387":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"6388":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#fnv1a32","content":"fnv1a32"},"6389":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#partitionfor","content":"partitionFor"},"6390":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#the-signed-byte-trap","content":"The signed byte trap"},"6391":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#the-partitioner-conformance-vectors","content":"The partitioner conformance vectors"},"6392":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6393":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"6394":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6395":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","content":"Documentation for the FNV-1a partitioning mechanism used by clients to select partitions."},"6396":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The partitioning mechanism is responsible for mapping a record's key to a specific partition index. Because the broker's record format has no room for a key, the client must perform this calculation locally and send only the resulting partition number `partition.js:4-5`."},"6397":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#fnv1a32","content":"The `fnv1a32` function implements the 32-bit FNV-1a hash algorithm `partition.js:28`. Because JavaScript numbers are doubles, a standard multiplication would lose precision and fail to wrap around correctly after three bytes; therefore, `Math.imul` is used to ensure 32-bit integer multiplication with the required wraparound `partition.js:16-20`. The final result is converted to an unsigned 32-bit integer using the `>>> 0` bitwise operator `partition.js:34`."},"6398":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#partitionfor","content":"The `partitionFor` function takes a `Uint8Array` key and the number of available partitions to determine the target partition `partition.js:48`. It uses an unsigned remainder (modulo) to fold the 32-bit hash into the range `[0, partitions)` `partition.js:52`. The function throws a `RangeError` if the partition count is not a positive integer, as there must be at least one valid partition to select `partition.js:49-50`."},"6399":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#the-signed-byte-trap","content":"A critical edge case in cross-language implementation is the interpretation of bytes. While the Node.js client iterates over a `Uint8Array` which yields unsigned bytes, languages like Java or Kotlin treat bytes as signed `partition.js:31`. Specifically, the byte `0x80` must be treated as `128` (unsigned) rather than `-128` (signed) to ensure the hash remains consistent across different client implementations `README.md:74-75`. Failure to mask these values results in a client that passes ASCII tests but fails on non-ASCII keys `README.md:75-76`."},"6400":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#the-partitioner-conformance-vectors","content":"To ensure that any client implementation (regardless of language) produces the same partition for a given key, the project uses \"golden vectors\" `generate.py:101-105`. These vectors are generated by a second, independent implementation in Python to ensure that the Kotlin client is not simply being tested against itself `generate.py:5-6`. The harness checks both the 32-bit hash and the resulting partition index across various partition counts `generate.py:30`."},"6401":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"File"},"6402":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6403":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6404":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"`…/src/partition.js`"},"6405":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"`10-34`"},"6406":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"FNV constants and the `fnv1a32` hashing function"},"6407":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"`…/src/partition.js`"},"6408":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"`48-52`"},"6409":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"The `partitionFor` folding logic"},"6410":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"`…/vectors/generate.py`"},"6411":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"`21-28`"},"6412":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#key-files","content":"Python implementation of the algorithms used for verification"},"6413":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"`partitionFor` will throw a `RangeError` if the `partitions` argument is not a positive integer, preventing the client from sending a record to a non-existent partition `partition.js:49-50`."},"6414":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `fnv1a32` function relies on `Math.imul` not just for speed, but because it is the only way to achieve the specific 32-bit wraparound required by the FNV-1a specification in JavaScript `partition.js:16-19`."},"6415":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node/fnv-partitioning#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The broker never actually sees the key used for partitioning; it only receives the calculated partition number from the client `partition.js:4-5`."},"6416":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"page","content":"clients/node","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients/node"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node"},"6417":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6418":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#brokererror-and-protocolerror","content":"BrokerError and ProtocolError"},"6419":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#fnv-1a-partitioning","content":"FNV-1a Partitioning"},"6420":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#crc-32c-checksum-verification","content":"CRC-32C Checksum Verification"},"6421":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#connection-framing-and-pipelining","content":"Connection Framing and Pipelining"},"6422":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#topic-and-partition-management","content":"Topic and Partition Management"},"6423":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#consumer-polling-and-async-iteration","content":"Consumer Polling and Async Iteration"},"6424":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#fakebroker-for-conformance-testing","content":"FakeBroker for Conformance Testing"},"6425":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6426":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-node#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"6427":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6428":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#brokererror-and-protocolerror","content":"The module distinguishes between errors caused by the broker's logic and errors caused by the transport layer. A `BrokerError` is raised when the broker understands the request but declines it (e.g., `UNKNOWN_TOPIC_OR_PARTITION), as defined in [`errors.js:25-33`](https://github.com/youndie/booblik/blob/ef58254ca7be0c2e8c83b5ee75d4ce32647cf800/clients/node/src/errors.js#L25-L33). In contrast, a `ProtocolError` is raised when the bytes on the connection do not make sense, such as a bad length or a lost socket, as seen in [`errors.js:35-40\\`]\\(https\\://github.com/youndie/booblik/blob/ef58254ca7be0c2e8c83b5ee75d4ce32647cf800/clients/node/src/errors.js#L35-L40)."},"6429":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#brokererror-and-protocolerror","content":"More: BrokerError and ProtocolError"},"6430":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#fnv-1a-partitioning","content":"To ensure deterministic record placement, the client uses the FNV-1a hash algorithm to map keys to specific partitions. The `fnv1a32` function in `partition.js:28-35` implements this using `Math.imul` to ensure 32-bit integer wraparound, which is critical because standard JavaScript numbers are doubles. The `partitionFor` function then folds this hash into the available partition count `partition.js:48-52`."},"6431":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#fnv-1a-partitioning","content":"More: FNV-1a Partitioning"},"6432":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#crc-32c-checksum-verification","content":"Data integrity is maintained via the CRC-32C (Castagnoli) polynomial `0x1EDC6F41` `crc32c.js:25`. The implementation in `crc32c.js:25-38` uses a pre-computed table for performance. Because JavaScript bitwise operators produce signed 32-bit results, the implementation uses `>>> 0` to ensure the final result is an unsigned 32-bit integer, preventing mismatches with the broker `crc32c.js:57`."},"6433":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#crc-32c-checksum-verification","content":"More: CRC-32C Checksum Verification"},"6434":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#connection-framing-and-pipelining","content":"The `Connection` class manages the lifecycle of a socket and supports pipelining by using a FIFO queue of pending requests `connection.js:50`. Each request is assigned a `correlationId` to ensure that responses are matched to the correct caller, even if they arrive out of order relative to the request stream `connection.js:141-153`. The framing logic uses a 4-byte length prefix to delineate messages `connection.js:97-105`."},"6435":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#topic-and-partition-management","content":"The client provides high-level abstractions for interacting with topics. The `topic` method in `connection.js:280-284` retrieves metadata from the broker to determine available partitions. Once a `Topic` is obtained, the `partitionFor` method handles routing: it uses the FNV-1a hash for keyed records or a round-robin approach for null keys `connection.js:311-316`."},"6436":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#consumer-polling-and-async-iteration","content":"The `Consumer` class manages the reading of a partition, tracking the `position` (the offset of the next record to be read) `consumer.js:111`. It provides an async iterator via the `records()` method, which uses `poll()` to fetch batches of data `consumer.js:189-192`. If a fetch returns a record that is larger than the `maxBytes` limit, a `RecordExceedsMaxBytesError` is thrown to prevent infinite retry loops `consumer.js:156-161`."},"6437":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#fakebroker-for-conformance-testing","content":"The `FakeBroker` class in `clients/node/test/fake-broker.js` is used to verify that the client correctly encodes requests and decodes responses. Unlike a real broker, it is designed to decode the client's payload to ensure the client's encoding is valid, rather than just pattern-matching bytes `fake-broker.js:5-7`. It can also simulate data corruption to test the client's checksum verification `fake-broker.js:178`."},"6438":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"File"},"6439":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6440":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6441":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`…/src/errors.js`"},"6442":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`3-10`"},"6443":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"The `Code` object containing broker refusal constants."},"6444":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`…/src/partition.js`"},"6445":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`28-35`"},"6446":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"The `fnv1a32` function for 32-bit FNV-1a hashing."},"6447":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`…/src/crc32c.js`"},"6448":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`25`"},"6449":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"The `POLYNOMIAL` constant for CRC-32C."},"6450":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`…/src/connection.js`"},"6451":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-34","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`141-153`"},"6452":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-35","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"The `#send` method for framing and sending requests."},"6453":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-36","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`…/src/consumer.js`"},"6454":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`105-120`"},"6455":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-38","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"The `Consumer` class constructor and properties."},"6456":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-39","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`…/test/fake-broker.js`"},"6457":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-40","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"`17-40`"},"6458":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-41","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#key-files","content":"The `FakeBroker` class definition."},"6459":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-42","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `partitionFor` function in `Topic` uses a round-robin counter that advances on every call when the key is null, meaning that calling `partitionFor` multiple times without sending a record will cause the client to skip partitions `connection.js:305-307`."},"6460":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-43","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `Consumer.poll` method advances the `position` by the number of records returned, but if a fetch is truncated, the partial record is dropped and the next poll starts from the beginning of that same record `consumer.js:139-142`."},"6461":{"id":"/wiki/clients-node-44","page_id":"/wiki/clients-node","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-node#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"In `Connection.#onData`, if a response's `correlationId` does not match the `correlationId` of the oldest pending request, the client rejects the request with a `ProtocolError` rather than attempting to find the correct match `connection.js:120-122`."},"6462":{"id":"/wiki/conformance","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"page","content":"conformance","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","conformance"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance"},"6463":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-0","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"6464":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-1","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6465":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-2","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"The conformance harness contract"},"6466":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-3","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance#the-partitioner-and-checksum-algorithms","content":"The partitioner and checksum algorithms"},"6467":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-4","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance#the-wire-protocol-reference-implementation","content":"The wire protocol reference implementation"},"6468":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-5","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"Producer and consumer verification scenarios"},"6469":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-6","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-test-runner","content":"The conformance test runner"},"6470":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-7","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6471":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-8","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"6472":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-9","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6473":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-10","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `conformance` module provides a suite of tools to ensure that any implementation of the booblik protocol—regardless of the programming language—adheres strictly to the specification. It focuses on catching \"silent\" failures: bugs that allow a client to run and appear functional while actually losing data, miscalculating partitions, or failing to handle specific edge cases like truncated network tails."},"6474":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-11","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"The harness interacts with the client via a command-line interface where the client is invoked as `<command> <verb> [args...]` (`client.py:28`). The interaction is based on a specific set of verbs and expected answers:"},"6475":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-12","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"Verb"},"6476":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-13","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"Arguments"},"6477":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-14","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"Answers"},"6478":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-15","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`capabilities`"},"6479":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-16","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"—"},"6480":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-17","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`roles=producer[,consumer]`, `name=<what to call it>`"},"6481":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-18","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`metadata`"},"6482":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-19","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`<topic>`"},"6483":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-20","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`partition=<id> <logStartOffset> <highWatermark>`"},"6484":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-21","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`produce`"},"6485":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-22","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`<topic> <partition> <ack> <hex>[,<hex>…]`"},"6486":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-23","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`baseOffset=`, `logEndOffset=`"},"6487":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-24","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`produce-keyed`"},"6488":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-25","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`<topic> <keyHex> <payloadHex>`"},"6489":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-26","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`partition=`, `baseOffset=`"},"6490":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-27","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`fetch`"},"6491":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-28","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`<topic> <partition> <offset> <maxBytes>`"},"6492":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-29","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"`highWatermark=`, `record=<hex>`"},"6493":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-30","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-harness-contract","content":"The client must write `key=value` lines to stdout, and any non-zero exit code from the client is treated as a failure (`client.py:81-83`)."},"6494":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-31","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-partitioner-and-checksum-algorithms","content":"The module implements two critical algorithms that are never sent over the wire but must be identical across all clients to ensure data consistency. These are defined in `algorithms.py:10-54`:"},"6495":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-32","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-partitioner-and-checksum-algorithms","content":"**FNV-1a**: An unsigned 32-bit hash used for partitioning (`algorithms.py:28-33`)."},"6496":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-33","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-partitioner-and-checksum-algorithms","content":"**CRC-32C (Castagnoli)**: A checksum used to verify record integrity, using the polynomial `0x1EDC6F41` (`algorithms.py:18-25`)."},"6497":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-34","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-partitioner-and-checksum-algorithms","content":"**Java Array Hash**: A specific implementation of `java.util.Arrays.hashCode(byte[])` that treats bytes as signed values (`algorithms.py:36-46`)."},"6498":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-35","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-partitioner-and-checksum-algorithms","content":"More: The partitioner and checksum algorithms"},"6499":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-36","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-wire-protocol-reference-implementation","content":"The `Connection` class in `wire.py:62-170` serves as a reference implementation of the protocol. It handles the mechanics of:"},"6500":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-37","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-wire-protocol-reference-implementation","content":"**Framing**: Requests are prefixed with a length, an API key, a version, and a correlation ID (`wire.py:81-83`)."},"6501":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-38","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-wire-protocol-reference-implementation","content":"**Decoding**: The `decode_records` function splits a payload into individual records and verifies the CRC of every complete record (`wire.py:173-196`)."},"6502":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-39","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-wire-protocol-reference-implementation","content":"**Error Handling**: The `ProtocolError` class handles error codes such as `UNKNOWN_TOPIC_OR_PARTITION` or `OFFSET_OUT_OF_RANGE` (`wire.py:29-36`)."},"6503":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-40","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-wire-protocol-reference-implementation","content":"More: The wire protocol reference implementation"},"6504":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-41","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"The harness executes specific scenarios to catch silent failures, as detailed in `scenarios.py:43-284`:"},"6505":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-42","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"**Producer Checks:**"},"6506":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-43","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"`metadata_matches`: Ensures reported partitions match the broker's actual state (`scenarios.py:72-78`)."},"6507":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-44","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"`record_round_trip`: Verifies bytes are not altered in transit (`scenarios.py:81-92`)."},"6508":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-45","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"`empty_record_is_refused`: Ensures zero-length records trigger a `CORRUPT_REQUEST` (`scenarios.py:95-115`)."},"6509":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-46","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"`ack_none_does_not_wait`: Verifies that `ack=none` returns immediately without waiting for a response (`scenarios.py:131-148`)."},"6510":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-47","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"`partitioner_matches_vectors`: The core check ensuring the client's partition choice matches the golden vectors (`scenarios.py:158-185`)."},"6511":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-48","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"**Consumer Checks:**"},"6512":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-49","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"`fetch_round_trip`: Verifies that fetched records match what was produced (`scenarios.py:207-218`)."},"6513":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-50","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"`truncated_tail`: Ensures a response that stops mid-record is handled by dropping the fragment (`scenarios.py:232-249`)."},"6514":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-51","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#producer-and-consumer-verification-scenarios","content":"`record_larger_than_max_bytes`: Verifies that a record exceeding `maxBytes` is reported via `recordExceedsMaxBytes` rather than causing a stall (`scenarios.py:252-281`)."},"6515":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-52","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-test-runner","content":"The `run.py` script manages the lifecycle of the test suite (`run.py:40-93`). It performs the following steps:"},"6516":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-53","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-test-runner","content":"Loads the partitioner vectors from a TSV file (`run.py:20-37`)."},"6517":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-54","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-test-runner","content":"Initializes the `Client` and calls `load_capabilities` to determine which roles to test (`run.py:51-58`)."},"6518":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-55","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-test-runner","content":"Iterates through all registered `CHECKS` in `scenarios.py`, skipping those for which the client has not declared a role (`run.py:65-70`)."},"6519":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-56","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#the-conformance-test-runner","content":"Reports the final count of passed, failed, and skipped checks (`run.py:84-86`)."},"6520":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-57","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"File"},"6521":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-58","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6522":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-59","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6523":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-60","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`…/harness/algorithms.py`"},"6524":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-61","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`10-54`"},"6525":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-62","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"Implementation of FNV-1a, Java array hash, and CRC-32C."},"6526":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-63","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`…/harness/client.py`"},"6527":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-64","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`51-113`"},"6528":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-65","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"The `Client` class for driving the command-line interface."},"6529":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-66","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`…/harness/scenarios.py`"},"6530":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-67","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`43-301`"},"6531":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-68","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"The list of specific test scenarios and the `Context` class."},"6532":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-69","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`…/harness/run.py`"},"6533":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-70","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`40-93`"},"6534":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-71","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"The main entry point for running the conformance suite."},"6535":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-72","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`…/harness/wire.py`"},"6536":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-73","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"`62-209`"},"6537":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-74","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#key-files","content":"The reference implementation of the wire protocol."},"6538":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-75","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**Silent Stalls**: A consumer that encounters a truncated tail or a record larger than `maxBytes` without implementing the specific error handling logic will stall forever, appearing to the user as if it has simply caught up to the end of the log (`scenarios.py:232-281`)."},"6539":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-76","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**The `ack=none` Trap**: In `produce` requests, `ack=none` means the broker sends absolutely nothing back; a client that waits for a response will block indefinitely (`scenarios.py:131-148`)."},"6540":{"id":"/wiki/conformance-77","page_id":"/wiki/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**Partitioning Sensitivity**: Using a partition count that is a power of two can mask errors in the partitioner because the low bits of the hash and the partition ID may align by chance (`README.md:103-108`)."},"6541":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"page","content":"The partitioner and checksum algorithms","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","conformance"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and"},"6542":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-0","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6543":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-1","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-partitioner","content":"The partitioner"},"6544":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-2","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-fnv1a32-and-partition_fnv1a-algorithms","content":"The `fnv1a32` and `partition_fnv1a` algorithms"},"6545":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-3","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-java_array_hash-and-partition_java_array_hash-algorithms","content":"The `java_array_hash` and `partition_java_array_hash` algorithms"},"6546":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-4","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-crc32c-checksum","content":"The `crc32c` checksum"},"6547":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-5","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-segmentwriter-checksum-verification","content":"The `SegmentWriter` checksum verification"},"6548":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-6","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-responsereader-checksum-verification","content":"The `ResponseReader` checksum verification"},"6549":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-7","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-conformance-vectors","content":"The conformance vectors"},"6550":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-8","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6551":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-9","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"6552":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-10","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6553":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-11","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-partitioner","content":"Mechanics of key distribution and the divergence between FNV-1a and Java-style hashing."},"6554":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-12","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-partitioner","content":"The partitioner determines which partition a record is assigned to based on its key. Because the key never reaches the broker—the client picks the partition and sends the number—any divergence in how two clients hash the same key results in data being placed in different partitions, breaking per-key ordering. This is a silent failure that the conformance suite is designed to catch `README.md:15-17`."},"6555":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-13","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-fnv1a32-and-partition_fnv1a-algorithms","content":"Implementation of unsigned 32-bit FNV-1a and its unsigned remainder folding."},"6556":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-14","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-fnv1a32-and-partition_fnv1a-algorithms","content":"The `fnv1a32` function implements FNV-1a over unsigned bytes using a specific offset basis and prime `algorithms.py:30-33`. The `partition_fnv1a` function then performs an unsigned remainder operation to map the hash to a partition count `algorithms.py:63`."},"6557":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-15","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-java_array_hash-and-partition_java_array_hash-algorithms","content":"Mechanics of signed byte summation and `Math.floorMod` folding, including the 0x80 sign-extension edge case."},"6558":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-16","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-java_array_hash-and-partition_java_array_hash-algorithms","content":"The `java_array_hash` function mimics `java.util.Arrays.hashCode(byte[])` by treating bytes as signed values; specifically, a byte like `0x80` enters the sum as `-128` rather than `128` `algorithms.py:39-45`. The `partition_java_array_hash` function uses `to_signed32` to ensure the hash is treated as a signed 32-bit integer before applying the modulo operator, which behaves like `Math.floorMod` for positive divisors `algorithms.py:68`."},"6559":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-17","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-crc32c-checksum","content":"Castagnoli polynomial implementation and the distinction from standard CRC-32."},"6560":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-18","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-crc32c-checksum","content":"The `crc32c` function implements the Castagnoli polynomial (`0x1EDC6F41`) using a reflected table implementation `algorithms.py:52-54`. It is explicitly noted that this is not `zlib.crc32` or other common CRC-32 implementations, as they use different polynomials `algorithms.py:50`."},"6561":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-19","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-segmentwriter-checksum-verification","content":"The role of CRC32C in protecting the log against torn records during recovery."},"6562":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-20","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-segmentwriter-checksum-verification","content":"In the `SegmentWriter` interface, every record is framed with a `CRC_BYTES` length checksum `SegmentWriter.kt:82`. This checksum is used during recovery to detect \"torn\" records—data that was only partially written when a process died—by verifying that the bytes match the header `SegmentWriter.kt:71-73`."},"6563":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-21","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-responsereader-checksum-verification","content":"Client-side verification of records on the zero-copy read path and `CorruptRecordException` handling."},"6564":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-22","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-responsereader-checksum-verification","content":"Because the broker uses zero-copy to stream bytes directly to the socket, the client is the only party that can verify the data integrity `ResponseReader.kt:36-37`. If a record's checksum fails during unpacking, the `ResponseReader` catches the protocol-level error and throws a `CorruptRecordException` `ResponseReader.kt:94-99`."},"6565":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-23","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-conformance-vectors","content":"Testing edge cases like non-ASCII keys, high bytes (0x80), and empty payloads."},"6566":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-24","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#the-conformance-vectors","content":"The conformance suite uses specific keys to catch common implementation errors, such as `0x80` which is the first byte where signed and unsigned readings diverge `generate.py:48-49`. It also tests various payload types including empty payloads, high bytes, and UTF-8 sequences like emoji or Cyrillic `generate.py:64-73`."},"6567":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-25","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"File"},"6568":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-26","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6569":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-27","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6570":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-28","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"`…/harness/algorithms.py`"},"6571":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-29","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"`28-68`"},"6572":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-30","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"Implementations of FNV-1a, Java-style array hashing, and CRC32C."},"6573":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-31","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"`…/storage/SegmentWriter.kt`"},"6574":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-32","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"`88-96`"},"6575":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-33","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"The `checksum` companion function for calculating CRC32C."},"6576":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-34","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"`…/vectors/generate.py`"},"6577":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-35","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"`117-120`"},"6578":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-36","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"Generation of CRC32C vectors for testing."},"6579":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-37","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"`…/client/ResponseReader.kt`"},"6580":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-38","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"`39-42`"},"6581":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-39","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#key-files","content":"Definition of `CorruptRecordException`."},"6582":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-40","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"A client that fails to verify checksums on the read path silently disables the project's only defense against log corruption `README.md:18-20`."},"6583":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-41","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `ResponseReader` might throw a `CorruptRecordException` with an offset that is relative to the start of the fetch response, not the absolute log position `ResponseReader.kt:96-97`."},"6584":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and-42","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-partitioner-and#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"An \"absurd\" frame length (e.g., `Int.MAX_VALUE`) is handled by dropping the connection rather than allowing the broker to attempt a massive allocation `PartialFrameTest.kt:112-118`."},"6585":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"page","content":"The wire protocol reference implementation","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","conformance"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol"},"6586":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-0","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6587":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-1","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-connection-framing-and-length-prefixing","content":"The Connection framing and length prefixing"},"6588":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-2","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#request-decoding-and-decoderesult","content":"Request decoding and `DecodeResult`"},"6589":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-3","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-session-protocol-loop","content":"The `Session` protocol loop"},"6590":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-4","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-fetchmode-and-zero-copy-path","content":"The `FetchMode` and zero-copy path"},"6591":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-5","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#handling-partial-frames-and-connection-survival","content":"Handling partial frames and connection survival"},"6592":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-6","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-awaitrecords-mechanism","content":"The `awaitRecords` mechanism"},"6593":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-7","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6594":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-8","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#behaviour-that-does-surprise","content":"Behaviour that does surprise"},"6595":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-9","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6596":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-10","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-connection-framing-and-length-prefixing","content":"The protocol uses a length-prefixed framing mechanism where each message is preceded by a 4-byte integer indicating the size of the following frame `Protocol.kt:24`. In the Python reference implementation, the `_send` method packs the API key, version, and correlation ID into a header before sending the payload, all wrapped in a length prefix `wire.py:81-82`. The `_receive` method performs the inverse, first reading the 4-byte length and then the exact number of bytes required to complete the frame `wire.py:86-87`."},"6597":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-11","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#request-decoding-and-decoderesult","content":"Decoding is a two-stage process designed to protect the broker. First, the `RequestDecoder` attempts to parse the header to identify the `ApiKey` and `correlationId` `Requests.kt:79-96`. If the header is valid, the decoder proceeds to parse the body; if the header itself is malformed, it returns a `DecodeResult.Failed` containing an `UNKNOWN_CORRELATION_ID` to prevent the broker attempting to respond to an invalid ID `Requests.kt:97-98`."},"6598":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-12","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-session-protocol-loop","content":"The `Session` class manages the lifecycle of a single connection through a continuous loop in the `serve` function `Session.kt:47-53`. The loop follows a strict sequence:"},"6599":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-13","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-session-protocol-loop","content":"`readFrame()`: Reads the next complete frame from the connection `Session.kt:50`."},"6600":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-14","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-session-protocol-loop","content":"`handle(frame)`: Dispatches the decoded request `Session.kt:51`."},"6601":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-15","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-session-protocol-loop","content":"`respondError` or `writeFully`: Sends the response back to the client `booblik-net/src/main/kotlin/ru/workinprogress/booblik/net/Session.kt:88, 151`.\nThis loop ensures that requests are served strictly in order, one at a time, to maintain consistency with the client's expectation of response ordering `Session.kt:30-34`."},"6602":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-16","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-fetchmode-and-zero-copy-path","content":"The broker supports two distinct modes for transferring data during a `fetch` request `Session.kt:219-230`:"},"6603":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-17","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-fetchmode-and-zero-copy-path","content":"Mode"},"6604":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-18","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-fetchmode-and-zero-copy-path","content":"Description"},"6605":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-19","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-fetchmode-and-zero-copy-path","content":"`FetchMode.ZERO_COPY`"},"6606":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-20","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-fetchmode-and-zero-copy-path","content":"Uses `connection.transferFrom` to move data directly from the log segment to the socket `Session.kt:221`"},"6607":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-21","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-fetchmode-and-zero-copy-path","content":"`FetchMode.HEAP`"},"6608":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-22","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-fetchmode-and-zero-copy-path","content":"Reads data into a reusable `staging` buffer before writing to the connection `Session.kt:225`"},"6609":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-23","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#handling-partial-frames-and-connection-survival","content":"The protocol is designed to be robust against network fragmentation. A frame may be split across multiple TCP packets; the `Session` handles this by reading the full length of the frame before attempting to decode it `Session.kt:68-71`. However, if a frame claims an \"absurd\" length that exceeds `Protocol.MAX_FRAME_BYTES`, the broker throws a `CorruptRequestException` and drops the connection to protect its memory `booblik-net/src/main/kotlin/ru/workinprogress/booblik/net/Session.kt:69, booblik-protocol/src/commonMain/kotlin/ru/workinprogress/booblik/net/wire/Protocol.kt:31`. Tests in `PartialFrameTest.kt:112-128` verify that while split packets are assembled correctly, invalid lengths result in connection closure."},"6610":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-24","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#the-awaitrecords-mechanism","content":"When a `FetchRequest` includes a `maxWaitMillis` and `minBytes` requirement, the broker enters a suspension loop in `awaitRecords` `Session.kt:246-267`. Instead of busy-waiting, the coroutine suspends using `handle.writer.highWatermark.first { it > seen }`, which resumes only when the log's watermark advances `Session.kt:261`. This ensures the broker does not waste CPU cycles while waiting for more data to become available in the log."},"6611":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-25","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"File"},"6612":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-26","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6613":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-27","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6614":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-28","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"`…/wire/Protocol.kt`"},"6615":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-29","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"`15-58`"},"6616":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-30","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"Protocol constants and API Key/Error Code definitions"},"6617":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-31","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"`…/net/Session.kt`"},"6618":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-32","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"`37-298`"},"6619":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-33","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"The main protocol loop and request handling logic"},"6620":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-34","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"`…/wire/Requests.kt`"},"6621":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-35","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"`79-184`"},"6622":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-36","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#key-files","content":"Request data structures and the `RequestDecoder`"},"6623":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-37","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#behaviour-that-does-surprise","content":"**Silent Silence**: In `produce` requests, if the `ackPolicy` is set to `ACK_NONE`, the broker returns no response at all rather than an empty response, because an offset does not exist until the writer reaches the batch `wire.py:149-150`."},"6624":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-38","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#behaviour-that-does-surprise","content":"**The Metadata Exception**: Unlike other requests, `MetadataRequest` is dispatched before the topic and partition are even parsed, because it is the only request that does not name a specific partition `Requests.kt:110-115`."},"6625":{"id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol-39","page_id":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/conformance/the-wire-protocol#behaviour-that-does-surprise","content":"**Clamped Waiting**: If a client requests a `maxWaitMillis` longer than 60 seconds, the broker clamps the wait to `MAX_FETCH_WAIT_MILLIS` to prevent holding coroutines and sockets open indefinitely `Protocol.kt:44`."},"6626":{"id":"/wiki/dev","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"page","content":"dev","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev"},"6627":{"id":"/wiki/dev-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"6628":{"id":"/wiki/dev-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6629":{"id":"/wiki/dev-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#layer-1-partitioned-event-stream-with-consumer-side-position","content":"Layer 1: Partitioned event stream with consumer-side position"},"6630":{"id":"/wiki/dev-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#layer-2-task-queue-via-claims-log-arbitration","content":"Layer 2: Task queue via claims log arbitration"},"6631":{"id":"/wiki/dev-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#layer-3-log-based-projection-and-state-reconstruction","content":"Layer 3: Log-based projection and state reconstruction"},"6632":{"id":"/wiki/dev-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#layer-4-kafka-to-booblik-relaying","content":"Layer 4: Kafka-to-booblik relaying"},"6633":{"id":"/wiki/dev-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#build-and-environment-configuration","content":"Build and environment configuration"},"6634":{"id":"/wiki/dev-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6635":{"id":"/wiki/dev-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"6636":{"id":"/wiki/dev-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6637":{"id":"/wiki/dev-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `dev` module provides a comprehensive demonstration of the `booblik` ecosystem through four architectural layers, ranging from basic partitioned event streams to complex Kafka-to-booblik relaying. It includes the necessary orchestration via `docker compose` and a suite of validation scripts to assert the correctness of the implementation."},"6638":{"id":"/wiki/dev-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-1-partitioned-event-stream-with-consumer-side-position","content":"This layer demonstrates how a publisher can distribute events across partitions using a key to ensure all events for a specific user land in the same partition, as described in `README.md:19-22`. The consumer is responsible for its own state, using `FileOffsetStore` to persist its position in a file on a volume to ensure at-least-once delivery guarantees (`README.md:30-33`). This mechanism ensures that if a process restarts, it can resume from its last known position, though it may replay a small number of records if the offset was not saved atomically (`README.md:35-38`)."},"6639":{"id":"/wiki/dev-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-1-partitioned-event-stream-with-consumer-side-position","content":"More: Layer 1: Partitioned event stream with consumer-side position"},"6640":{"id":"/wiki/dev-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-2-task-queue-via-claims-log-arbitration","content":"This layer implements a coordination-free task queue where workers compete for tasks by appending claims to a `claims` topic (`README.md:68-70`). Instead of a central coordinator, the order of the log acts as the arbiter; the first claim to land in a partition wins the lease (`README.md:69-70`). A lease is determined by a timestamp written into the claim itself, ensuring that workers with clock skew still reach the same conclusion about whether a lease has expired (`README.md:72-75`). This design avoids the need for a central coordinator but results in a growing claims log and requires workers to read every claim (`README.md:88-92`)."},"6641":{"id":"/wiki/dev-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-3-log-based-projection-and-state-reconstruction","content":"The projection layer treats state as a pure function of the log, where the service stores nothing and instead builds its view by replaying the log (`README.md:129-131`). The lifecycle of a projection is managed through two distinct phases: `replay()` which catches up to the high watermark, and `follow()` which maintains the view as new events arrive (`README.md:133-136`). To prevent data corruption, the service does not persist its position separately from the state; instead, it rebuilds the entire view from the beginning to ensure consistency (`README.md:141-144`)."},"6642":{"id":"/wiki/dev-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-4-kafka-to-booblik-relaying","content":"This layer provides bidirectional translation between Kafka and the `booblik` protocol, acting as a bridge between ecosystems (`README.md:164-166`). The relay is a single module that changes its behavior based on the environment, specifically regarding who is responsible for remembering the position:"},"6643":{"id":"/wiki/dev-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-4-kafka-to-booblik-relaying","content":"Direction"},"6644":{"id":"/wiki/dev-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-4-kafka-to-booblik-relaying","content":"Who remembers the position"},"6645":{"id":"/wiki/dev-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-4-kafka-to-booblik-relaying","content":"Kafka $\\to$ booblik"},"6646":{"id":"/wiki/dev-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-4-kafka-to-booblik-relaying","content":"Kafka, in a consumer group"},"6647":{"id":"/wiki/dev-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-4-kafka-to-booblik-relaying","content":"booblik $\\to$ Kafka"},"6648":{"id":"/wiki/dev-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-4-kafka-to-booblik-relaying","content":"`FileOffsetStore` on a volume"},"6649":{"id":"/wiki/dev-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#layer-4-kafka-to-booblik-relaying","content":"(`README.md:174-175`). While per-key ordering is preserved during the crossing, the Kafka key itself is not stored in the `booblik` wire format and cannot be recovered on the way back (`README.md:177-180`)."},"6650":{"id":"/wiki/dev-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#build-and-environment-configuration","content":"The project uses Gradle with Kotlin DSL, where `subprojects` are configured to maintain a consistent style using `ktlint` (`build.gradle.kts:13-18`). A critical configuration detail is that `repositories { }` blocks defined in subprojects will replace, rather than append to, the repositories defined in `settings.gradle.kts` due to the `PREFER_PROJECT` mode (`build.gradle.kts:21-24`). Additionally, the Docker images are explicitly built for the `amd64` platform to avoid manifest errors on `arm64` hosts (`README.md:209-210`)."},"6651":{"id":"/wiki/dev-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"File"},"6652":{"id":"/wiki/dev-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6653":{"id":"/wiki/dev-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6654":{"id":"/wiki/dev-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"6655":{"id":"/wiki/dev-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-224`"},"6656":{"id":"/wiki/dev-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Detailed documentation of the four architectural layers and known defects."},"6657":{"id":"/wiki/dev-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/build.gradle.kts`"},"6658":{"id":"/wiki/dev-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-37`"},"6659":{"id":"/wiki/dev-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Gradle configuration for subprojects, including plugins and toolchains."},"6660":{"id":"/wiki/dev-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/check-projection.sh`"},"6661":{"id":"/wiki/dev-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-50`"},"6662":{"id":"/wiki/dev-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Script to validate the projection's ability to rebuild state via replay."},"6663":{"id":"/wiki/dev-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/check-queue.sh`"},"6664":{"id":"/wiki/dev-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-20`"},"6665":{"id":"/wiki/dev-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Script to assert that tasks are won by exactly one worker."},"6666":{"id":"/wiki/dev-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/check-redistribution.sh`"},"6667":{"id":"/wiki/dev-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-68`"},"6668":{"id":"/wiki/dev-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Script to test task redistribution after a worker is killed."},"6669":{"id":"/wiki/dev-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/check-relay.sh`"},"6670":{"id":"/wiki/dev-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-50`"},"6671":{"id":"/wiki/dev-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Script to verify the full round-trip of records through the relays."},"6672":{"id":"/wiki/dev-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Position Persistence**: The `Projection` service does not persist its position because persisting a position without the state it belongs to can lead to silent corruption; it must rebuild everything from the log to be certain of its state (`README.md:141-144`)."},"6673":{"id":"/wiki/dev-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Task Redistribution**: In the queue implementation, a task is not \"released\" by a worker shutting down gracefully; instead, the task becomes claimable again simply because the lease in the log expires (`check-redistribution.sh:7-9`)."},"6674":{"id":"/wiki/dev-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Repository Overriding**: Declaring `repositories { }` in a subproject does not add to the parent repositories but replaces them, which can lead to \"Could not find\" errors if the parent repositories are lost (`build.gradle.kts:21-24`)."},"6675":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","type":"page","content":"Layer 1: Partitioned event stream with consumer-side position","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event"},"6676":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6677":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#the-key-to-partition-mapping","content":"The Key-to-Partition Mapping"},"6678":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#fileoffsetstore-and-atomic-position-persistence","content":"FileOffsetStore and Atomic Position Persistence"},"6679":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#at-least-once-guarantee-and-checkpointing","content":"At-least-once Guarantee and Checkpointing"},"6680":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#recovery-and-the-resumedfrom-field","content":"Recovery and the resumedFrom Field"},"6681":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#validation-via-checksh","content":"Validation via check.sh"},"6682":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6683":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"6684":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6685":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#the-key-to-partition-mapping","content":"The client is responsible for the routing logic. Every event is associated with a key (such as a user ID), and the `TopicHandle.partitionFor` function hashes this key to determine a specific partition number (`Main.kt:79`). Crucially, the key itself is never sent over the wire; only the resulting partition number is transmitted in the protocol (`README.md:20`). This ensures that all events for a single user land in the same partition, guaranteeing that a single consumer handles them in the correct order (`README.md:21`)."},"6686":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#fileoffsetstore-and-atomic-position-persistence","content":"To ensure that a consumer's position survives a crash, the `FileOffsetStore` implements persistence using a file on a dedicated volume (`FileOffsetStore.kt:28`). To prevent data corruption during a crash, the `save` operation does not overwrite the existing file directly. Instead, it writes the new offset to a temporary file and then performs an atomic move using `StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE` (`FileOffsetStore.kt:57`). This prevents a \"half-saved\" position, which would be parsed as a truncated number and cause silent replay of multiple records (`FileOffsetStore.kt:24`)."},"6687":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#at-least-once-guarantee-and-checkpointing","content":"The system provides an at-least-once delivery guarantee through the specific ordering of the `checkpointing` operation. The consumer handles the batch of records first and only saves the offset to the `OffsetStore` **after** the collector has successfully processed the batch (`Main.kt:83`). If a crash occurs between the handling of the records and the saving of the offset, the consumer will replay the batch upon restart (`README.md:36`)."},"6688":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#recovery-and-the-resumedfrom-field","content":"When a consumer restarts, it attempts to resume from its last known position. It uses `StartPosition.At(offset)` if a saved offset exists, otherwise it defaults to `StartPosition.Earliest` to read from the beginning of the live log (`Main.kt:68`). The `Stats` class tracks this via the `resumedFrom` field, which is populated with the saved offset if a restart occurs, allowing for visibility into whether the consumer is starting from a historical point or from the beginning (`Main.kt:69`)."},"6689":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#validation-via-checksh","content":"The correctness of the consumer's recovery logic is verified by `check.sh`. The test suite considers it legal for a consumer to resume slightly behind its last known position, as this is a natural consequence of the at-least-once guarantee (`README.md:37`). However, the test will fail if the consumer attempts to start from the very beginning of the log when it should have resumed from a specific offset (`README.md:38`)."},"6690":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"File"},"6691":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6692":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6693":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"`…/common/FileOffsetStore.kt`"},"6694":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"`53-57`"},"6695":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"Implementation of atomic position saving via temporary files and moves."},"6696":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"6697":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"`77-88`"},"6698":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"The main consumer loop including `follow`, `checkpointing`, and record handling."},"6699":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"6700":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"`30-33`"},"6701":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#key-files","content":"Documentation on `FileOffsetStore` and the risks of truncated offsets."},"6702":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Silent Replay:** Because of the way `FileOffsetStore` handles file writes, a truncated file (e.g., `\"12\"` becoming `\"1\"`) is still a valid long, which causes the consumer to silently replay eleven records (`FileOffsetStore.kt:24`)."},"6703":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Key Erasure:** While the client uses a key to select a partition, the key itself is not part of the booblik wire protocol and is not stored or transmitted (`README.md:20`)."},"6704":{"id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/layer-partitioned-event#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Earliest vs. Zero:** Using `StartPosition.Earliest` does not necessarily mean starting from offset zero; it means starting from the beginning of the *live* log, which depends on the current retention settings (`Main.kt:65`)."},"6705":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"page","content":"Partitioning by key and consumer-side position","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and"},"6706":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6707":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#the-keyed-partitioner","content":"The Keyed Partitioner"},"6708":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#fileoffsetstore","content":"FileOffsetStore"},"6709":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#at-least-once-checkpointing","content":"At-least-once Checkpointing"},"6710":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"Recovery after a crash"},"6711":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#the-split-assertion","content":"The split assertion"},"6712":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6713":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"6714":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6715":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#the-keyed-partitioner","content":"The client uses a key to determine which partition a record belongs to before the record is sent. As described in `README.md:19-21`, the `TopicHandle` hashes the key and sends the broker a partition number; the key itself never reaches the wire. This ensures that all events for a specific user are routed to the same partition, allowing a single service to handle them in order."},"6716":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#fileoffsetstore","content":"To ensure that a consumer's position survives a process restart, the `FileOffsetStore` implements `OffsetStore` by writing the position to a file on a volume. As detailed in `README.md:30-32`, this is achieved through a temporary file and an atomic move to prevent corruption. This mechanism ensures that a \"half-saved\" position is not written, which would otherwise cause silent replays of truncated offsets."},"6717":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#at-least-once-checkpointing","content":"The system guarantees at-least-once delivery by managing the lifecycle of a batch carefully. According to `README.md:35-37`, `checkpointing` saves the offset only **after** the collector has successfully handled the batch. If a failure occurs between the handling of the batch and the saving of the offset, the batch will be replayed upon restart."},"6718":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"When a consumer restarts, it uses its persisted position to resume reading. The `resumedFrom` field in the consumer's `/stats` endpoint provides visibility into this process (`README.md:52-53`). As noted in `check.py:14-16`, resuming slightly **behind** the last known position is considered legal behavior because the at-least-once guarantee allows for the replay of a batch that was handled but not yet checkpointed."},"6719":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#the-split-assertion","content":"The integrity of the partitioning and consumption is validated by an external check. The `check.sh` script triggers a validation where `check.py` asserts that every record sent by the publisher reaches exactly one consumer (`README.md:44-47`). The `split` mode in `check.py:25-38` verifies that the number of records written to a partition matches the position held by the consumer assigned to that partition."},"6720":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"File"},"6721":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6722":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6723":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"6724":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`19-22`"},"6725":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Mechanics of keyed partitioning and partition assignment."},"6726":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"6727":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`30-33`"},"6728":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Implementation details of `FileOffsetStore` and atomic moves."},"6729":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"6730":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`35-38`"},"6731":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Explanation of at-least-once checkpointing logic."},"6732":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"6733":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`52-53`"},"6734":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Behavior of the `resumedFrom` field during restarts."},"6735":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/check.py`"},"6736":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`25-38`"},"6737":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Logic for asserting partition/position matches."},"6738":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/check.sh`"},"6739":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`40-48`"},"6740":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Orchestration of the split assertion check."},"6741":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Silent Replays**: Because checkpointing happens after processing, a consumer might start from a position slightly behind its last successful work, causing it to replay the last batch (`README.md:36-37`)."},"6742":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Key Erasure**: A Kafka key does not survive the crossing into booblik; while it is used to pick the partition on the way in, it is not stored in the booblik wire format and cannot be recovered on the way back (`README.md:177-180`)."},"6743":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Non-monotonicity in `partitionFor(null)`**: Calling `partitionFor(null)` advances an internal counter, meaning that checking which partition a record *would* go to before actually sending it can cause records to skip partitions (`README.md:204-206`)."},"6744":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"page","content":"State as a fold over the log","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over"},"6745":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6746":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-lifecycle","content":"The Projection lifecycle"},"6747":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"The Projection state and Progress"},"6748":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#rebuilt-view-and-the-replay-guarantee","content":"Rebuilt view and the replay guarantee"},"6749":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6750":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"6751":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6752":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-lifecycle","content":"The projection transitions from a historical reconstruction to a live stream through two distinct phases. First, it performs a `replay()` from the `Earliest` position to catch up with history, a process that ends when the stream reaches the high watermark as it was when the service started (`Main.kt:98-104`). Once the replay is complete, the service enters the `follow()` phase, which does not end, allowing the view to stay current (`Main.kt:108-128`). The seamless transition between these two phases is achieved via `RecordBatch.nextOffset`, which ensures the `follow` starts exactly where the `replay` stopped, preventing gaps or double-counting (`Main.kt:36-39`)."},"6753":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"The projection is designed such that the service stores nothing locally; its state is purely a functional fold over the log (`Main.kt:28-30`). To avoid silent corruption, the service deliberately does not persist its position; persisting a position without the corresponding state would result in a service that resumes at a late offset with an empty view (`Main.kt:40-42`). The current status of the service is exposed via `ProjectionStats`, which includes:"},"6754":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Field"},"6755":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Description"},"6756":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`replayComplete`"},"6757":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Whether the historical replay has finished"},"6758":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`live`"},"6759":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Whether the service is currently following the tail"},"6760":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`fromReplay`"},"6761":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Total events processed during the replay phase"},"6762":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`fromFollow`"},"6763":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Total events processed during the follow phase"},"6764":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`applied`"},"6765":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Total events applied to the projection"},"6766":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`users`"},"6767":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Total number of unique users in the view"},"6768":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"(`Main.kt:176-188`)"},"6769":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#rebuilt-view-and-the-replay-guarantee","content":"When a service restarts, it must rebuild its entire state from the log. The `rebuilt` mode in the testing suite asserts that a restarted projection must return at least as many events as it had before the restart (`check.py:193-207`). A failure is triggered if the `applied` count is less than the previous state or if the `fromReplay` count does not match the expected history, ensuring that the view is not left incomplete (`check.py:209-211`)."},"6770":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"File"},"6771":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6772":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"What is there"},"6773":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"6774":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`44-77`"},"6775":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"The main entry point and HTTP routing for the projection service"},"6776":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"6777":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`79-143`"},"6778":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"The `build` function managing the replay and follow lifecycles"},"6779":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"6780":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`145-188`"},"6781":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"The `Progress` class and `ProjectionStats` data structure"},"6782":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`dev/check.py`"},"6783":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`170-189`"},"6784":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"Logic for asserting the integrity of the projection view"},"6785":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Non-persistence of position**: The `Projection` class does not persist its position because doing so without the state would lead to silent corruption (`Main.kt:40-42`)."},"6786":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `replayComplete` signal**: The transition from history to live data is signaled by the `replayComplete` flag, which is set only after the `replay` flow completes (`Main.kt:105`)."},"6787":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**At-least-once semantics**: Because the position is moved only after the consumer handles a batch, a crash between handling and saving will cause the batch to be replayed upon restart (`README.md:35-37`)."},"6788":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"page","content":"Atomic offset persistence","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/common"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence"},"6789":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6790":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#fileoffsetstore","content":"FileOffsetStore"},"6791":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"The write-ahead-log and SegmentMode"},"6792":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"Recovery after a crash"},"6793":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruption-detection-and-checksums","content":"Corruption detection and checksums"},"6794":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"CorruptionTest"},"6795":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"Key files"},"6796":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"Behaviour that surprising"},"6797":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"6798":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#fileoffsetstore","content":"The mechanism for persisting consumer positions using atomic file moves to prevent corruption during crashes. As described in `FileOffsetStore.kt:23-25`, a position saved halfway is considered worse than no position at all, as a truncated offset like `\"1\"` parsed from `\"12\"` would cause silent replays. To prevent this, the `save` method writes to a temporary file and uses `StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE` to ensure the offset is updated completely or not at all `FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`."},"6799":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"The system provides two distinct write paths for log segments, which can be selected via the `SegmentMode` enum `LogSegment.kt:25`:"},"6800":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"Mode"},"6801":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"Description"},"6802":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"`FILE_CHANNEL`"},"6803":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"Uses a standard `FileChannel` for writing."},"6804":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"`MAPPED`"},"6805":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"Uses memory mapping for the write path."},"6806":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"The `MAPPED` mode is the default and is chosen based on performance measurements `LogSegment.kt:19-20`. While `MAPPED` can be faster, it can suffer from performance degradation when the writer outruns the OS writeback, a phenomenon explored in `SustainedWriteProbe.kt:18-19`."},"6807":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"When a `LogSegment` is opened, it must undergo a recovery process to rebuild the `SparseOffsetIndex` by walking the record headers `LogSegment.kt:253-254`. The recovery mechanism reads headers in chunks using a `RECOVERY_BUFFER` to avoid excessive syscalls `LogSegment.kt:327-330`. Because recovery relies on the length prefix, a record whose declared length extends beyond the file boundary is discarded, and the segment is truncated to the last intact boundary `LogSegment.kt:302-304`."},"6808":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruption-detection-and-checksums","content":"To address the risk where a torn write inside a record body is not detectable by length prefixes alone, the system implements checksums (M-60) `LogSegment.kt:258-259`. During recovery, the `verify` function checks the record's body against its stored CRC `LogSegment.kt:405`. If a mismatch is detected, the recovery process stops to prevent the system from treating garbage as valid data `LogSegment.kt:364-366`."},"6809":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"The `CorruptionTest` class validates that the system correctly identifies and reacts to data corruption `CorruptionTest.kt:23`. Key test cases include:"},"6810":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"**Bit-flipping**: Ensuring a single bit flip in a record body is caught `CorruptionTest.kt:50-51`."},"6811":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"**Torn bodies**: Verifying that even if a length prefix survives, a modified body is detected `CorruptionTest.kt:108-109`."},"6812":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"**Recovery boundaries**: Ensuring that once a bad record is encountered, the system stops and does not allow subsequent records to be incorrectly processed `CorruptionTest.kt:64-65`."},"6813":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"File"},"6814":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"Lines"},"6815":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-26"