t the fastest setting; it sends every record individually, which is significantly slower than batching (`README.md:10-12`)."},"6995":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java-78","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"`AckPolicy.NONE`: This mode is the only one where the broker may drop an accepted record silently (`README.md:63-64`)."},"6996":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java-79","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"`Consumer`: The `hasNext()` method is always true and blocks until data is available because a partition has no defined end (`README.md:101-102`)."},"6997":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"page","content":"Producer Configuration and Batching","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","clients/java"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and"},"6998":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-0","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"6999":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-1","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"ProducerConfig"},"7000":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-2","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#the-accumulator","content":"The Accumulator"},"7001":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-3","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#ackpolicynone","content":"AckPolicy.NONE"},"7002":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-4","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#the-batch-mechanism","content":"The `batch` mechanism"},"7003":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-5","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#the-close-lifecycle-and-_fail_leftovers","content":"The `close` lifecycle and `_fail_leftovers`"},"7004":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-6","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#race-conditions-in-the-accumulation-loop","content":"Race conditions in the accumulation loop"},"7005":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-7","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerlostrecordtest","content":"ProducerLostRecordTest"},"7006":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-8","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7007":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-9","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7008":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-10","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7009":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-11","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"The configuration for the producer determines how the accumulator behaves."},"7010":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-12","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"Parameter"},"7011":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-13","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"Type"},"7012":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-14","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"Description"},"7013":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-15","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"`maxBatchSize`"},"7014":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-16","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"`int`"},"7015":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-17","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"The maximum number of records per request; reaching this triggers an immediate send (`Producer.kt:23`)."},"7016":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-18","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"`lingerMillis` / `linger`"},"7017":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-19","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"`long` / `float`"},"7018":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-20","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"The time a batch waits for more records; zero is not the fast setting, as it sends every record individually (`ProducerConfig.java:15`)."},"7019":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-21","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"`ack` / `ackPolicy`"},"7020":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-22","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"`AckPolicy`"},"7021":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-23","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerconfig","content":"Determines the acknowledgment behavior (`producer.py:32`)."},"7022":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-24","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#the-accumulator","content":"The accumulator is the core performance driver of the client. A single record per request is highly inefficient; for example, batches of a hundred can reach 4,335,482 records/s compared to only 80,592 for single records (`README.md:37`). The accumulation loop uses a windowing logic where the deadline is calculated from the arrival of the **first** record in a batch, not the last, to prevent a steady trickle of records from postponing a send indefinitely (`Producer.kt:139`)."},"7023":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-25","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#ackpolicynone","content":"When `AckPolicy.NONE` is selected, the client does not wait for a broker response to confirm the record's existence. Because no offset is assigned until the broker's writer reaches the batch, the client returns `OFFSET_UNKNOWN` (or `Offset.ZERO` in some implementations) to remain honest about the state of the record (`Producer.kt:205`)."},"7024":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-26","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#the-batch-mechanism","content":"The `batch` function provides a way to bypass the accumulator entirely. This is used to send a group of records as a single request, guaranteeing that the records land contiguously in the partition with consecutive offsets (`Publishing.kt:74`)."},"7025":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-27","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#the-close-lifecycle-and-_fail_leftovers","content":"During shutdown, the producer must ensure no records are left hanging. The `close` method flushes queued data, and if any records remain in the mailbox after the loop has terminated, `_fail_leftovers` (or `drainPending`) is called to fail those records with an exception so the caller is not left waiting forever (`producer.py:108`)."},"7026":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-28","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#race-conditions-in-the-accumulation-loop","content":"A critical edge case occurs when a timeout and a new record arrival happen simultaneously. Using a standard `receive` with a timeout can cause a \"cancelled receive\" that swallows a record, leaving the caller's `CompletableDeferred` hanging (`Producer.kt:143`). The implementation uses `select` to ensure that the `mailbox` either takes the element or takes the timeout, but never both or neither, preventing data loss (`Producer.kt:154`)."},"7027":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-29","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#producerlostrecordtest","content":"This test verifies the integrity of the accumulator by simulating a scenario where two different topics with different arrival rates are sent through a single producer (`ProducerLostRecordTest.kt:52`). It ensures that the accumulator does not drop records when the timing of a record arrival coincides with the expiration of the linger window."},"7028":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-30","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"File"},"7029":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-31","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7030":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-32","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7031":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-33","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"`…/java/ProducerConfig.java`"},"7032":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-34","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"`14-16`"},"7033":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-35","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"Default producer configuration factory."},"7034":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-36","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"`…/client/Producer.kt`"},"7035":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-37","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"`139-146`"},"7036":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-38","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"Logic regarding the accumulation window and `select` usage."},"7037":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-39","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"`…/client/Publishing.kt`"},"7038":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-40","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"`74-79`"},"7039":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-41","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"Guarantees regarding contiguous offsets in a `batch`."},"7040":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-42","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"`…/booblik/producer.py`"},"7041":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-43","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"`108-112`"},"7042":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-44","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#key-files","content":"Handling of leftover records during producer closure."},"7043":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-45","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`Producer.send` is not immediate; it returns a handle to a result that will only be completed once the accumulator decides to flush the batch (`Producer.kt:75`)."},"7044":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-46","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`AckPolicy.NONE` results in `OFFSET_UNKNOWN` because the broker has not yet assigned an offset to a record that hasn't been processed by the writer (`producer.py:20`)."},"7045":{"id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and-47","page_id":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/clients-java/producer-configuration-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`Producer.batch` is not an atomic transaction; a crash during the write can result in a partial batch surviving on disk (`Publishing.kt:76`)."},"7046":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"page","content":"consumeForever","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/consumer"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever"},"7047":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"7048":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7049":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#the-consumeforever-lifecycle","content":"The `consumeForever` lifecycle"},"7050":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#at-least-once-delivery-via-checkpointing","content":"At-least-once delivery via `checkpointing`"},"7051":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#recovery-from-broker-disconnection","content":"Recovery from broker disconnection"},"7052":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#the-stats-snapshot-mechanism","content":"The `Stats` snapshot mechanism"},"7053":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7054":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"7055":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7056":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `consumeForever` function serves as the primary execution engine for the consumer service. It orchestrates the continuous reading of a specific partition from a Booblik topic, manages the lifecycle of the network connection to the broker, and ensures that the consumer's progress is persisted to local storage to allow for seamless restarts."},"7057":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#the-consumeforever-lifecycle","content":"The lifecycle begins by initializing a `FileOffsetStore` (`Main.kt:60`) and determining the starting position for the stream. As seen in `Main.kt:60-68`, the consumer attempts to load a previously saved offset from the `stateDir`; if no state exists, it defaults to `StartPosition.Earliest`. Once the starting position is determined, the function enters an infinite `while(true)` loop (`Main.kt:75`) that manages the `BooblikSubscriber` connection. The subscriber uses the `follow` method (`Subscription.kt:120`) to stream `RecordBatch` objects from the broker to the consumer."},"7058":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#at-least-once-delivery-via-checkpointing","content":"Data integrity is maintained through a specific execution order in the `checkpointing` operator. As implemented in `Subscription.kt:260-265`, the `checkpointing` extension function wraps the collection process. It ensures that the `store.save` operation occurs only **after** the `emit(batch)` call has successfully passed the data to the collector. This ensures that if the `handle` function (`Main.kt:100-107`) fails or the process crashes, the offset is not updated, causing the next run to replay the same batch, thus guaranteeing at-least-once delivery."},"7059":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#recovery-from-broker-disconnection","content":"The consumer is designed to be resilient to network instability. If the connection to the broker is lost, the `BooblikSubscriber` throws an exception which is caught by the `try-catch` block in `Main.kt:89-95`. Instead of terminating, the loop logs the failure, increments the `reconnects` metric in the `Stats` object (`Main.kt:93`), waits for 1000ms (`Main.kt:94`), and attempts to re-establish the connection. Upon reconnection, the consumer uses the last successfully saved offset via `StartPosition.At(it)` (`Main.kt:68`) to resume reading exactly where it left off."},"7060":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#the-stats-snapshot-mechanism","content":"Real-time observability is provided by the `Stats` class, which maintains the current state of the consumer. The `observe` function (`Main.kt:125-131`) is called for every batch to update the `position` (the next expected offset) and the `lag` (the distance from the high watermark). These values, along with the `lastRecord` and `reconnects` count, are captured in a `ConsumerStats` data class via the `snapshot` method (`Main.kt:133-144`) to be served over HTTP."},"7061":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"File"},"7062":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7063":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7064":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"7065":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`56-97`"},"7066":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"The `consumeForever` orchestration loop and error handling."},"7067":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"7068":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`109-145`"},"7069":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"The `Stats` class and `ConsumerStats` data structure."},"7070":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`…/client/Subscription.kt`"},"7071":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`260-265`"},"7072":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"The `checkpointing` extension function for at-least-once delivery."},"7073":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**At-least-once semantics**: Because `checkpointing` saves the offset *after* the batch is emitted to the collector (`Subscription.kt:263`), a crash during the `handle` function will result in the same batch being processed again upon restart."},"7074":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**Connection ownership**: A `BooblikSubscriber` owns its own connection per partition; if you follow multiple partitions, `BooblikSubscriber` will open a separate connection for each to avoid head-of-line blocking (`Subscription.kt:157-158`)."},"7075":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**Non-blocking health checks**: The `Health` check does not use a simple TCP connect because a successful handshake does not guarantee the broker is actually processing requests; instead, it performs a full `metadata` request (`Health.kt:60-61`)."},"7076":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"page","content":"dev/consumer","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/consumer"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer"},"7077":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7078":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"ConsumerConfig"},"7079":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumeforever","content":"consumeForever"},"7080":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"Recovery after a crash"},"7081":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#reconnection-logic","content":"Reconnection logic"},"7082":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#stats-and-monitoring","content":"Stats and monitoring"},"7083":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7084":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"7085":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-16, sources: 2. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7086":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Configuration parameters are loaded from environment variables to define how the consumer connects to the broker and where it stores its state, as implemented in `Main.kt:160-181`."},"7087":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Environment Variable"},"7088":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Default Value"},"7089":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Description"},"7090":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`CONSUMER_NAME`"},"7091":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`consumer`"},"7092":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Name of the consumer instance"},"7093":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`BOOBLIK_HOST`"},"7094":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`127.0.0.1`"},"7095":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Broker host address"},"7096":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`BOOBLIK_PORT`"},"7097":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`9092`"},"7098":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Broker port"},"7099":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`BOOBLIK_TOPIC`"},"7100":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`events`"},"7101":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"The topic to subscribe to"},"7102":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`BOOBLIK_PARTITION`"},"7103":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`0`"},"7104":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"The specific partition to consume"},"7105":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`STATE_DIR`"},"7106":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`/var/lib/consumer`"},"7107":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Directory for `FileOffsetStore`"},"7108":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`HTTP_PORT`"},"7109":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`8080`"},"7110":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Port for the metrics HTTP server"},"7111":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumeforever","content":"The main execution loop, implemented in `Main.kt:56-97`, maintains a continuous connection to the broker. It uses a `while(true)` loop to ensure that if a `BooblikSubscriber` fails or the broker becomes unavailable, the consumer attempts to re-establish the subscription."},"7112":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumeforever","content":"More: consumeForever"},"7113":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"To ensure at-least-once delivery, the consumer uses `FileOffsetStore` to persist the last successfully processed offset. Upon startup, `consumeForever` checks for a saved offset in `Main.kt:67-68`; if found, it resumes from that position; otherwise, it starts from `StartPosition.Earliest`. The checkpointing mechanism is applied via `.checkpointing(store)` in `Main.kt:83`, ensuring the offset is saved only after the batch has been processed."},"7114":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#reconnection-logic","content":"Connectivity issues are handled within a `try-catch` block inside the loop in `Main.kt:76-95`. If an exception occurs (such as the broker going away), the consumer increments the `reconnects` counter, waits for a 1000ms delay, and then attempts to reconnect."},"7115":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#reconnection-logic","content":"More: Reconnection logic"},"7116":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#stats-and-monitoring","content":"The service exposes operational metrics through an embedded Ktor server configured in `Main.kt:46-52`. The `Stats` class tracks the number of handled records, the current position, the lag, and the number of reconnection attempts, which are then serialized to JSON via the `/stats` endpoint."},"7117":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"File"},"7118":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7119":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7120":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/build.gradle.kts`"},"7121":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`1-8`"},"7122":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"Dependency declarations for Ktor, Booblik client, and serialization"},"7123":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"7124":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`38-54`"},"7125":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-48","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"The `main` function entry point and server setup"},"7126":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-49","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"7127":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-50","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`56-97`"},"7128":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-51","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"The `consumeForever` loop and subscription logic"},"7129":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-52","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"7130":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-53","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`109-145`"},"7131":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-54","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"The `Stats` class and `ConsumerStats` data class"},"7132":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-55","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"7133":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-56","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`160-181`"},"7134":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-57","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"The `ConsumerConfig` data class and environment loading"},"7135":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-58","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `checkpointing` function in `consumeForever` is designed such that the offset is saved **after** the `collect` block finishes, which guarantees at-least-once delivery by replaying batches if a crash occurs before the save is completed (`Main.kt:80-82`)."},"7136":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-59","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `StartPosition.Earliest` setting used in `consumeForever` refers to the start of the **live** log (the data currently retained by the broker) rather than the absolute beginning of the log history (`Main.kt:64-66`)."},"7137":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"page","content":"Reconnection logic","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/consumer"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic"},"7138":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7139":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-boobliksubscriber-lifecycle","content":"The `BooblikSubscriber` lifecycle"},"7140":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-booblikconnection-and-session-failure-modes","content":"The `BooblikConnection` and `Session` failure modes"},"7141":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-lastacceptfailure-and-lastsessionfailure-metrics","content":"The `lastAcceptFailure` and `lastSessionFailure` metrics"},"7142":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-sessionstresstest-pipelined-exchange","content":"The `SessionStressTest` pipelined exchange"},"7143":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7144":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7145":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7146":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-boobliksubscriber-lifecycle","content":"The consumer's main loop is designed to be resilient to broker unavailability. As seen in `Main.kt:75-96`, the `while(true)` loop wraps the entire `BooblikSubscriber` lifecycle. If the broker goes away, the `catch (failure: Exception)` block intercepts the error, increments the `reconnects` counter in the `Stats` class (`Main.kt:93`), and waits for 1000ms before attempting to re-establish the connection. This ensures that a broker restart does not crash the consumer service, as the consumer maintains its state via the `FileOffsetStore` (`Main.kt:60`)."},"7147":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-booblikconnection-and-session-failure-modes","content":"The system distinguishes between different levels of failure to provide better diagnostic data. While `Consumer.kt:89-91` shows that a `poll()` can throw a `FetchFailedException` when the broker returns a non-zero `ErrorCode`, there are deeper failures. A `Session` might die due to an exception while a request is being handled, which is a distinct event from a client simply leaving a connection. This distinction is critical for identifying bugs in the server's request handling logic (`Metrics.kt:55-67`)."},"7148":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-lastacceptfailure-and-lastsessionfailure-metrics","content":"The broker provides specific metrics to help operators distinguish between \"the broker is unreachable\" and \"the broker is running but failing to handle requests.\" According to `Metrics.kt:45-67`, the `lastAcceptFailure` captures exceptions occurring in the `accept` loop, which can lead to a \"silent death\" where the process is alive but no new connections can be made. Meanwhile, `lastSessionFailure` captures exceptions that kill a specific session while it is actively holding a request, providing evidence for intermittent failures that would otherwise be invisible (`Metrics.kt:55-67`)."},"7149":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-sessionstresstest-pipelined-exchange","content":"To ensure the stability of the pipelined exchange, `SessionStressTest.kt:27-52` runs a high-volume loop of produce and receive operations. This test is designed to catch rare, non-deterministic failures (like the \"M-64\" issue) by repeating the exchange hundreds of times. A key concern during these tests is ephemeral port exhaustion; if the test environment runs out of local ports due to many connections stuck in `TIME_WAIT`, the test will fail not because of a broker bug, but because the harness has exhausted the OS resources (`SessionStressTest.kt:97-101`)."},"7150":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"File"},"7151":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7152":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7153":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"7154":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`75-96`"},"7155":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"The infinite retry loop and reconnection delay logic."},"7156":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`…/net/Metrics.kt`"},"7157":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`45-67`"},"7158":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"Volatile fields for tracking the last accept and session failures."},"7159":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`…/client/Consumer.kt`"},"7160":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`89-91`"},"7161":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"Logic for throwing `FetchFailedException` on broker errors."},"7162":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`…/net/SessionStressTest.kt`"},"7163":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`27-52`"},"7164":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"The stress test harness for repeated connection cycles."},"7165":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Silent Accept Failures**: If an exception occurs in the `accept` loop, the broker might continue to run and hold its port, but it will stop accepting any new connections. This is tracked via `lastAcceptFailure` in `Metrics.kt:50`."},"7166":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**At-Least-Once via Checkpointing**: The `checkpointing` mechanism in `Main.kt:83` is intentionally placed after the `collect` block. This means if a crash occurs after processing a batch but before saving the offset, the batch will be replayed upon restart."},"7167":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `RecordExceedsMaxBytesException`**: A consumer might stop making progress if a single record is larger than the `maxBytes` configured in `Consumer.kt:28-35`. This is a client-side limit that results in a permanent failure to advance the position."},"7168":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"page","content":"Recovery after a crash","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/consumer"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash"},"7169":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7170":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#fileoffsetstore-and-atomic-position-updates","content":"FileOffsetStore and atomic position updates"},"7171":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#logsegment-recovery-and-index-rebuilding","content":"LogSegment recovery and index rebuilding"},"7172":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"SegmentMode and data integrity boundaries"},"7173":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#partitionlog-segment-continuity","content":"PartitionLog segment continuity"},"7174":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#the-at-least-once-guarantee-in-consumer-and-relay","content":"The at-least-once guarantee in consumer and relay"},"7175":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#validation-of-resumed-positions","content":"Validation of resumed positions"},"7176":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7177":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7178":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7179":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#fileoffsetstore-and-atomic-position-updates","content":"To prevent corruption of the consumer's state, `FileOffsetStore` avoids overwriting the current offset file directly. Instead, it writes the new offset to a temporary file and uses an atomic move to replace the original, ensuring that a crash during the write process does not leave a truncated or corrupted offset file (`FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`)."},"7180":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#logsegment-recovery-and-index-rebuilding","content":"When a broker restarts, it must reconstruct its internal lookup structures. The `LogSegment.open` function allows the system to reopen existing segments, verifying that the `nextOffset` and the index are consistent with the data stored on disk (`RecoveryTest.kt:37-38`)."},"7181":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"The behavior of the recovery depends on the `SegmentMode` used:"},"7182":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"Mode"},"7183":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"Recovery Behavior"},"7184":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"`FILE_CHANNEL`"},"7185":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"The file length acts as a boundary; a record with a header claiming more bytes than the file contains is discarded (`RecoveryTest.kt:78-79`)."},"7186":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"`MAPPED`"},"7187":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"The file length is pre-sized, so recovery relies on the presence of a zero-length prefix to identify the end of the log (`RecoveryTest.kt:100-101`)."},"7188":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#partitionlog-segment-continuity","content":"A `PartitionLog` is composed of multiple segments. Upon reopening, the system restores the continuity of the partition by identifying all existing segments and maintaining their order, ensuring the `nextOffset` reflects the total count across all segments (`RecoveryTest.kt:117-118`)."},"7189":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#the-at-least-once-guarantee-in-consumer-and-relay","content":"The system provides at-least-once delivery by ensuring that the position is only updated after the work is completed. In `consumeForever`, the `checkpointing` operator saves the offset only after the `collect` block (which includes `handle`) has finished (`Main.kt:83-84`). Similarly, the relay ensures that Kafka is flushed before the booblik position is updated (`Directions.kt:119`)."},"7190":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#validation-of-resumed-positions","content":"The system distinguishes between a successful recovery and a failure based on the starting point. A consumer is considered to have failed if it starts from the beginning of the log (`README.md:37-38`), whereas resuming from a position slightly behind the last known offset is considered a valid replay of a batch (`check.py:54-55`)."},"7191":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"File"},"7192":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7193":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7194":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`…/common/FileOffsetStore.kt`"},"7195":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`55-57`"},"7196":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"The atomic move mechanism using a temporary file."},"7197":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"7198":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`83-84`"},"7199":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"The checkpointing logic that ensures at-least-once delivery."},"7200":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`…/relay/Directions.kt`"},"7201":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`119`"},"7202":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"The requirement to flush Kafka before checkpointing."},"7203":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`FileOffsetStore.save` uses a `.tmp` file to ensure that a crash during a write doesn't leave a \"half-saved\" position that could cause silent replays (`FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`)."},"7204":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"In `booblikToKafka`, the relay does not store the Kafka key; while it preserves per-key ordering by using the key to pick the booblik partition, the key itself is lost during the crossing (`Directions.kt:115-116`)."},"7205":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `check.py` script considers a consumer \"resumed\" even if it starts slightly behind its last known position, because replaying a batch is the expected behavior of an at-least-once system (`check.py:54-55`)."},"7206":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"page","content":"dev/projection","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/projection"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection"},"7207":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7208":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#projection","content":"Projection"},"7209":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#the-build-lifecycle","content":"The build lifecycle"},"7210":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#progress","content":"Progress"},"7211":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#recovery-after-a-broker-failure","content":"Recovery after a broker failure"},"7212":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7213":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#behaviour-that-should-surprise","content":"Behaviour that should surprise"},"7214":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 3. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7215":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#projection","content":"The core in-memory state machine that derives `UserView` from the event log. The `Projection` class manages a `ConcurrentHashMap` of users and uses `AtomicLong` to track applied and skipped events `View.kt:39-43`. It processes `Event` objects by updating user-specific statistics like event counts and action frequencies `View.kt:55-67`."},"7216":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#projection","content":"More: Projection"},"7217":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#the-build-lifecycle","content":"The transition from the replay stage to the follow stage to ensure a gapless view. The `build` function first executes a `replay` from `StartPosition.Earliest` to catch up with history `Main.kt:98`. Once the replay completes, the `progress.replayComplete` flag is set to true `Main.kt:105`, and the system transitions to a `follow` stage for each partition `Main.kt:112-130`. This ensures that the `follow` starts exactly at the `nextOffset` reported by the replay `Main.kt:117-119`."},"7218":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#the-build-lifecycle","content":"More: The build lifecycle"},"7219":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#progress","content":"Monitoring metrics including replay/follow counts, rebuild counts, and partition positions. The `Progress` class tracks the number of events from replay and follow stages using `AtomicLong` `Main.kt:146-147`, and maintains a map of current partition positions `Main.kt:149`."},"7220":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#recovery-after-a-broker-failure","content":"The mechanism for rebuilding the entire state from the beginning of the log when the connection is lost. If an exception occurs during the build loop, the `catch` block increments the rebuild count and resets the `live` and `replayComplete` flags `Main.kt:135-140`. The entire process then restarts, re-reading the log from the beginning to ensure state consistency `Main.kt:87-143`."},"7221"