views during the `follow` phase `View.kt:55`."},"7281":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"The `decode` function in `View.kt` uses `runCatching` to silently skip unreadable records, incrementing a `skipped` counter instead of crashing the projection `View.kt:48-53`."},"7282":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"The `top` function in `Projection` performs a full sort of all users in memory to return the requested limit `View.kt:71`."},"7283":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"The build lifecycle","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/projection"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle"},"7284":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"7285":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7286":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#the-replay-and-follow-transition","content":"The replay and follow transition"},"7287":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#the-statelessness-of-projection","content":"The statelessness of Projection"},"7288":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#rebuild-verification","content":"Rebuild verification"},"7289":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#at-least-once-semantics-in-checkpointing","content":"At-least-once semantics in checkpointing"},"7290":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7291":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7292":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7293":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `dev/projection` module implements a read model where state is a pure function of the event log. It is responsible for consuming a stream of events from a `BooblikSubscriber`, building an in-memory view, and providing an HTTP query surface."},"7294":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#the-replay-and-follow-transition","content":"To ensure the query surface transitions from \"building\" to \"current\" without losing or duplicating data, the projection uses a two-phase approach. First, it performs a `replay()` to consume history. The `RecordBatch.nextOffset` property is critical here; it reports where each partition stopped during the replay, allowing the subsequent `follow()` phase to start exactly at that offset `Main.kt:137-139`. This mechanism ensures that the view catches up to the high watermark without gaps or redundant processing."},"7295":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#the-statelessness-of-projection","content":"A core design principle is that the projection stores nothing on disk. While a consumer might persist its position, the projection's state is purely in-memory. As noted in the documentation, persisting a position without the corresponding state would lead to silent corruption `README.md:141-144`. Instead, upon any restart, the service must rebuild its entire state by replaying the log from the beginning to ensure the view is consistent with the truth of the log."},"7296":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#rebuild-verification","content":"The integrity of the rebuild process is verified by ensuring that a restarted service recovers at least the same amount of data it held previously. The `check-projection.sh` script performs this by capturing the `applied` count before a restart and asserting that the rebuilt view contains at least that many events `check-projection.sh:42-47`. This prevents a scenario where a service resumes from a late offset with an empty view, which would result in a \"successful\" but incorrect state."},"7297":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#at-least-once-semantics-in-checkpointing","content":"The system guarantees at-least-once delivery through the mechanics of `checkpointing`. The offset is only saved after the collector has successfully handled a batch of records `SubscriptionTest.kt:143-172`. If an error occurs during the `apply` phase, the checkpoint is not updated, meaning the next attempt will replay the same batch, ensuring no event is lost at the cost of potential duplicates."},"7298":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"File"},"7299":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7300":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7301":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"7302":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`145-173`"},"7303":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"The `Progress` class tracking replay/follow status and statistics."},"7304":{"id":"/wik