/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`151-217`"},"7663":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-55","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"The `work` loop in the worker, including the claim-write and settlement-check logic."},"7664":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-56","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/ClaimStateTest.kt`"},"7665":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-57","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`22-107`"},"7666":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-58","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"Unit tests for the `ClaimState` state machine."},"7667":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-59","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The Settlement Round Trip**: A worker does not consider itself the owner of a task immediately after sending a claim; it must wait until the claim is read back from the log to confirm it wasn't superseded by a race (`Main.kt:184-193`)."},"7668":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-60","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Clock Independence**: The `holds` function in `ClaimState` (`Claims.kt:95-98`) uses the timestamp stored in the record rather than the current system time, making the state machine deterministic across different machines."},"7669":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-61","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The \"Wasted\" Attempt**: In `Report.kt:82`, an attempt is considered \"lost\" if it was written to the log but did not result in a successful lease, representing work that happened because a worker was \"in step\" with others."},"7670":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"page","content":"ClaimState and the Deterministic Verdict","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/queue-worker"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the"},"7671":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7672":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"ClaimRecord and the Log Format"},"7673":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimstate-the-pure-function-of-the-log","content":"ClaimState: The Pure Function of the Log"},"7674":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#lease-expiry-and-the-timestamp-based-verdict","content":"Lease Expiry and the Timestamp-based Verdict"},"7675":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#the-claimable-mechanism-and-local-clock-skew","content":"The claimable Mechanism and Local Clock Skew"},"7676":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"Verification of Determinism in ClaimStateTest"},"7677":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7678":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"7679":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7680":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","content":"Documentation for the deterministic state machine used in the queue worker module."},"7681":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"The state of the queue is driven by a sequence of `ClaimRecord` entries written to a specific partition of the `claims` topic. As defined in `Claims.kt:14-21`, each record contains the worker's identity, the task ID, and a timestamp. The log format distinguishes between two primary types:"},"7682":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"Type"},"7683":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"Constant"},"7684":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"Description"},"7685":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"`claim`"},"7686":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"`ClaimRecord.CLAIM`"},"7687":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"An attempt by a worker to acquire a lease on a task."},"7688":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"`done`"},"7689":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"`ClaimRecord.DONE`"},"7690":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"A notification that a task has been successfully completed."},"7691":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimstate-the-pure-function-of-the-log","content":"The `ClaimState` class acts as a deterministic state machine. The \"verdict\" of the queue is a pure function of the log: by replaying all `ClaimRecord` entries in order using the `apply` function (`Claims.kt:62-92`), any worker can reconstruct the current state of all leases and completed tasks. Because `apply` only relies on the data within the records and not the reader's system clock, the state is perfectly reproducible across different machines."},"7692":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#lease-expiry-and-the-timestamp-based-verdict","content":"A critical design choice is how leases expire. Instead of using the reader's current time, the expiration is judged by the timestamp `at` written into the claim itself. As explained in `Claims.kt:48-51`, a claim is considered \"taken\" if the task is already in the `done` set or if the new claim's timestamp falls within the duration of an existing lease (`Claims.kt:77`). This ensures that if Worker A claims a task at $T=1000$ for 30s, Worker B's claim at $T=1031$ will win, even if Worker B's local clock thinks it is only $T=1010$."},"7693":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#the-claimable-mechanism-and-local-clock-skew","content":"While the final verdict is deterministic, workers still need to decide which tasks they *should* attempt to claim. This is handled by the `claimable` function (`Claims.kt:101-104`), which takes a `now: Long` parameter representing the reader's local clock. This function filters tasks that are not yet `done` and whose current lease has not yet lapsed according to the local time. This is the only place where clock skew matters: it affects *when* a worker decides to try for a task, but it never changes the outcome of who actually wins the task once the claim is written to the log."},"7694":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"The correctness of this logic is verified in `ClaimStateTest.kt`. The tests ensure that:"},"7695":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"The first claim in a sequence wins if subsequent claims land within the lease period (`ClaimStateTest.kt:45-50`)."},"7696":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"A claim arriving after a lease has lapsed successfully takes over the task (`ClaimStateTest.kt:53-60`)."},"7697":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"Once a task is marked as `DONE`, it cannot be claimed again (`ClaimStateTest.kt:63-69`)."},"7698":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"Unknown record types do not disturb existing leases but do advance the `consumedUpTo` offset (`ClaimStateTest.kt:100-107`)."},"7699":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"File"},"7700":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7701":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7702":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"7703":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`14-43`"},"7704":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"Definition of `ClaimRecord` and its serialization."},"7705":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"7706":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`57-103`"},"7707":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"The `ClaimState` state machine and lease logic."},"7708":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/ClaimStateTest.kt`"},"7709":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`44-107`"},"7710":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"Unit tests for the deterministic state machine."},"7711":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**`ClaimState.apply`** is a pure function that ignores the reader's clock; it only cares about the timestamps embedded in the `ClaimRecord` objects."},"7712":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**`claimable`** is the only function where the local clock is allowed to influence the logic, and it only affects the *attempt* to claim, not the *result* of the claim."},"7713":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**`ClaimRecord.decode`** uses `runCatching` to return `null` for unknown types, allowing the state machine to skip unknown records without crashing or losing its position in the log."},"7714":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"page","content":"dev/queue-worker","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/queue-worker"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker"},"7715":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"7716":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7717":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimrecord-and-the-claims-log","content":"ClaimRecord and the Claims Log"},"7718":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimstate-and-the-deterministic-verdict","content":"ClaimState and the Deterministic Verdict"},"7719":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"Worker Lifecycle and the Work Loop"},"7720":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Worker Metrics and Health Monitoring"},"7721":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#queue-report-and-observability","content":"Queue Report and Observability"},"7722":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7723":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7724":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 5. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7725":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `dev/queue-worker` module implements a distributed task queue protocol built on top of a log-based broker. Instead of relying on a centralized coordinator with complex locking mechanisms, this module uses a single partition of a `claims` topic as a deterministic arbiter. Workers compete for tasks by writing claims to the log; the order of these records in the log determines the winner."},"7726":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimrecord-and-the-claims-log","content":"The coordination relies on a sequence of `ClaimRecord` entries written to a specific topic. These records define the lifecycle of a task through two primary types: `CLAIM` and `DONE` (`Claims.kt:24-25`). Each record contains the worker's identity, the task ID, and a timestamp (`at`) representing the worker's wall clock at the time of the claim (`Claims.kt:18-19`)."},"7727":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimrecord-and-the-claims-log","content":"More: ClaimRecord and the Claims Log"},"7728":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimstate-and-the-deterministic-verdict","content":"The core logic of the queue is encapsulated in `ClaimState`, which acts as a pure function that folds over the log to produce a consistent view of task ownership (`Claims.kt:57`). A key design feature is that leases are judged using the timestamp written into the `ClaimRecord` itself, rather than the reader's local clock (`Claims.kt:50-51`). This ensures that all workers replaying the same log reach the exact same verdict regarding which worker holds a lease (`Claims.kt:76-85`)."},"7729":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimstate-and-the-deterministic-verdict","content":"More: ClaimState and the Deterministic Verdict"},"7730":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"The `work` function manages the continuous loop of task acquisition and execution (`Main.kt:151-217`). The lifecycle follows these steps:"},"7731":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Identify**: Find `claimable` tasks by checking the current `ClaimState` (`Main.kt:171`)."},"7732":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Claim**: Write a `CLAIM` record to the `claimsTopic` (`Main.kt:178`)."},"7733":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Settle**: Wait for the claim to appear in the log by observing `consumedUpTo` (`Main.kt:192`)."},"7734":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Execute**: Perform the work for the duration of `workMillis` (`Main.kt:207`)."},"7735":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Complete**: Write a `DONE` record to the log (`Main.kt:210`)."},"7736":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"More: Worker Lifecycle and the Work Loop"},"7737":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Each worker exposes an HTTP server via Ktor to provide observability into its internal state (`Main.kt:68`). The available endpoints are:"},"7738":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Endpoint"},"7739":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Method"},"7740":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Description"},"7741":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"`/health`"},"7742":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"GET"},"7743":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Returns \"ok\""},"7744":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"`/stats`"},"7745":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"GET"},"7746":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Returns `WorkerStats` including claim latency and task counts"},"7747":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"`/task/{offset}`"},"7748":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"GET"},"7749":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Returns `TaskState` for a specific task offset (`Main.kt:77`)"},"7750":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#queue-report-and-observability","content":"The `Report` object is a standalone tool used to audit the entire queue by replaying the `claims` and `tasks` topics from the beginning (`Report.kt:22`). It calculates the \"wasted\" attempts—claims that lost the race—to measure queue efficiency (`Report.kt:82`). It also performs a critical safety check: if the number of `DONE` records does not match the number of distinct tasks completed, it flags an error indicating a task was worked twice (`Report.kt:98`)."},"7751":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"File"},"7752":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7753":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7754":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue-worker/build.gradle.kts`"},"7755":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`1-7`"},"7756":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"Dependencies for Ktor, serialization, and logback"},"7757":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"7758":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`13-33`"},"7759":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`ClaimRecord` data class and JSON serialization logic"},"7760":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"7761":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`57-101`"},"7762":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`ClaimState` logic for replaying the log and determining leases"},"7763":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-48","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"7764":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-49","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`307-341`"},"7765":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-50","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`WorkerConfig` and environment variable mapping"},"7766":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-51","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Report.kt`"},"7767":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-52","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`22-65`"},"7768":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-53","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"Logic for replaying topics to generate a queue report"},"7769":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-54","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `claimable` function uses the local clock (`now`) to determine if a lease has lapsed, but the actual ownership verdict is determined by the timestamps stored within the `ClaimRecord` itself (`Claims.kt:101`)."},"7770":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-55","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `apply` function in `ClaimState` is designed to be a pure function, ensuring that the \"verdict\" is a deterministic result of the log order, regardless of when a worker reads it (`Claims.kt:63`)."},"7771":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-56","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"In `Main.kt`, a worker must wait for its own claim to \"settle\" (appear in the log) before it begins work, meaning the worker's view of the world is always slightly behind its own actions (`Main.kt:185`)."},"7772":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"page","content":"Worker Lifecycle and the Work Loop","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/queue-worker"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and"},"7773":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Table of Contents"},"7774":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7775":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#the-work-loop-and-task-acquisition","content":"The `work` loop and task acquisition"},"7776":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#the-claimstate-arbiter","content":"The `ClaimState` arbiter"},"7777":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#lease-expiry-and-the-heldat-mechanism","content":"Lease expiry and the `heldAt` mechanism"},"7778":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#redistribution-and-the-check-redistributionsh-scenario","content":"Redistribution and the `check-redistribution.sh` scenario"},"7779":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#workerstats-and-claim-latency","content":"`WorkerStats` and claim latency"},"7780":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7781":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7782":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7783":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"7784":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"The `work` loop and task acquisition"},"7785":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"The `ClaimState` arbiter"},"7786":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Lease expiry and the `heldAt` mechanism"},"7787":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Redistribution and the `check-redistribution.sh` scenario"},"7788":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"WorkerStats and claim latency"},"7789":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Key files"},"7790":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7791":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#the-work-loop-and-task-acquisition","content":"The main execution loop in `work` continuously attempts to acquire tasks by selecting a candidate from the `claimable` list (`Main.kt:171-172`). Taking a task is not an instantaneous operation; it requires a full round trip where the worker writes a claim and then waits for that claim to appear in its own view of the log (`Main.kt:187-193`). To mitigate collisions when many workers are idle, the `pickRandom` configuration allows workers to pick a random task from the claimable list instead of always picking the first one (`Main.kt:172`)."},"7792":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#the-claimstate-arbiter","content":"The `ClaimState` class acts as the arbiter of the queue by replaying the claims log to determine the current state of all tasks (`Claims.kt:46`). The verdict of who owns a task is a pure function of the log, implemented via the `apply` function, which updates the state based on `ClaimRecord` types (`Claims.kt:63-91`). This ensures that all workers reading the same log prefix reach the same conclusion regarding task ownership and completion."},"7793":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#lease-expiry-and-the-heldat-mechanism","content":"Leases are managed by comparing the timestamp written into a claim against the timestamp of subsequent claims. A lease is considered active if the current claim's timestamp is less than the lease's expiry time, calculated via `heldAt` (`Claims.kt:41`). Crucially, the expiry is judged by the timestamp recorded in the claim itself, not the reader's local clock, which prevents disagreement between workers with clock skew (`Claims.kt:52-54`)."},"7794":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#redistribution-and-the-check-redistributionsh-scenario","content":"The system handles worker failure through lease expiration. If a worker is killed via `SIGKILL` while holding a task, the task is not explicitly released; instead, it becomes `claimable` again once the lease expires in the log (`check-redistribution.sh:54-58`). The `check-redistribution.sh` script verifies that a surviving worker can successfully take over a task previously held by a \"victim\" worker (`check-redistribution.sh:58-62`)."},"7795":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#workerstats-and-claim-latency","content":"Monitoring is provided through `WorkerStats`, which tracks the number of attempts, wins, and losses (`Main.kt:275-288`). The `claimLatencyMicros` measures the time taken for the round trip between writing a claim and seeing it settled in the log (`Main.kt:198`). High collision rates are reflected in the ratio of `won` vs `lost` attempts, where \"lost\" attempts represent work that happened only because no one was there to claim the task (`Report.kt:82-83`)."},"7796":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"File"},"7797":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7798":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7799":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"7800":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`56-98`"},"7801":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"The `main` function and server setup"},"7802":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"7803":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`151-217`"},"7804":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"The `work` loop implementation"},"7805":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"7806":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`13-40`"},"7807":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`ClaimRecord` data class and serialization"},"7808":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"7809":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`57-91`"},"7810":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`ClaimState` logic and `apply` function"},"7811":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"7812":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`36-40`"},"7813":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`Lease` data class"},"7814":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"7815":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`219-272`"},"7816":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`Stats` class and `snapshot` method"},"7817":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"7818":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`307-341`"},"7819":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`WorkerConfig` and environment loading"},"7820":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `ClaimState.apply` function is a pure function that never reads a local clock, ensuring that the verdict is a deterministic result of the log content (`Claims.kt:63-91`)."},"7821":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-48","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"A worker's `claimable` tasks are determined by the `now` parameter passed into the function, which allows the worker to use its local clock to decide which tasks to *try* for, even though the *win* is decided by the log's timestamps (`Claims.kt:102-104`)."},"7822":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-49","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `Report` object calculates \"wasted\" attempts by subtracting `wins` from total `attempts`, which is a metric of how many claims were made for tasks already held or finished (`Report.kt:82-83`)."},"7823":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","type":"page","content":"Benchmark execution and reporting","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki",".github"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and"},"7824":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-0","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7825":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-1","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-maincibenchmark-mode","content":"The `mainCiBenchmark` mode"},"7826":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-2","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-benchmark-floorsh-mechanism","content":"The `benchmark-floor.sh` mechanism"},"7827":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-3","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-partitionwriterbenchmark-lifecycle","content":"The `PartitionWriterBenchmark` lifecycle"},"7828":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-4","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-startupprobe-and-recovery-scan","content":"The `StartupProbe` and recovery scan"},"7829":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-5","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-maincibenchmark-exclusion-rules","content":"The `mainCiBenchmark` exclusion rules"},"7830":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-6","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7831":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-7","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7832":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-8","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7833":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-9","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-maincibenchmark-mode","content":"The CI environment uses a specific configuration designed to filter out noise inherent to shared, hosted runners. Instead of running the full suite, it executes `mainCiBenchmark` as defined in `build.gradle.kts:97-110`, which focuses on tasks that are not heavily influenced by the non-deterministic performance of a hosted runner's disk."},"7834":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-10","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-benchmark-floorsh-mechanism","content":"To avoid the pitfalls of comparing runs from different machines—where a 20-40% swing is common—the system does not look for percentage-based regressions. Instead, `benchmark-floor.sh:24-26` implements a \"floor\" mechanism that only triggers if performance collapses by an order of magnitude. It compares the current throughput against a hardcoded threshold (e.g., 20,000 for `FILE_CHANNEL` and 1,000,000 for `MAPPED`) to ensure that only catastrophic failures, rather than minor runner variance, cause a build failure."},"7835":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-11","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-partitionwriterbenchmark-lifecycle","content":"The benchmark lifecycle is designed to measure the real-world cost of a broker's operations. In `PartitionWriterBenchmark.kt:98-102`, the `log.retainAtMost` call is explicitly included within the measured `@Benchmark` loop. This ensures that the overhead of log retention (cleaning up old segments) is accounted for in the throughput numbers, reflecting the actual performance a user would experience in a production environment."},"7836":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-12","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-startupprobe-and-recovery-scan","content":"The `StartupProbe` measures the efficiency of the system's recovery mechanism. As detailed in `StartupProbe.kt:41-49`, the probe measures the time taken to scan segments and rebuild the index. It performs three separate attempts to capture the difference between a \"cold\" restart (paying for the initial page cache miss) and \"warm\" restarts, providing a realistic range for how long a broker takes to recover after a crash."},"7837":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-13","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-maincibenchmark-exclusion-rules","content":"To maintain a stable CI signal, certain benchmarks are explicitly excluded from the `ci` configuration in `build.gradle.kts:111-117`. Specifically, `GroupCommitBenchmark` is excluded because its performance is heavily dependent on disk barriers and `fsync` latency, which vary wildly on hosted runners. Additionally, the `flushEveryAppend` parameter is set to `false` to avoid measuring paths that are primarily bounded by disk I/O latency rather than code efficiency."},"7838":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-14","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"File"},"7839":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-15","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7840":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-16","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7841":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-17","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/benchmark.yml`"},"7842":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-18","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`33-38`"},"7843":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-19","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"Workflow trigger configuration for scheduled and manual runs"},"7844":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-20","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/benchmark.yml`"},"7845":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-21","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`69-70`"},"7846":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-22","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"Execution of the `mainCiBenchmark` task"},"7847":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-23","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`booblik-benchmark/build.gradle.kts`"},"7848":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-24","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`111-117`"},"7849":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-25","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"Definition of the `ci` benchmark configuration and exclusions"},"7850":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-26","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`ci/benchmark-floor.sh`"},"7851":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-27","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`24-26`"},"7852":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-28","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"Hardcoded performance floor values for different modes"},"7853":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-29","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`…/benchmark/PartitionWriterBenchmark.kt`"},"7854":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-30","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`98-102`"},"7855":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-31","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"Inclusion of retention logic in the benchmark loop"},"7856":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-32","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`…/probe/StartupProbe.kt`"},"7857":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-33","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`41-49`"},"7858":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-34","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"Logic for measuring recovery scan time and throughput"},"7859":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-35","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Non-comparable numbers:** Because of runner variance, the system explicitly refuses to compare the current CI run to the previous one; it only checks if the current run is \"not a total collapse\" via `benchmark-floor.sh:70-71`."},"7860":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-36","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Intentional failure in `build.yml`:** The `build.yml:42-43` step compiles benchmarks but never runs them, ensuring that broken benchmarks are caught during the build phase without producing unreliable data."},"7861":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-37","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `allOpen` requirement:** In `build.gradle.kts:46-51`, the `allOpen` plugin is used to make `@State` classes non-final, which is necessary because JMH generates subclasses of these classes during execution."},"7862":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"Docker image lifecycle and GHCR publishing","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki",".github"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle"},"7863":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7864":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-release-workflow-and-tag-based-publishing","content":"The `release` workflow and tag-based publishing"},"7865":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-check-gate-and-distribution-integrity","content":"The `check` gate and distribution integrity"},"7866":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-docker-smoke-conformance-kit","content":"The `docker-smoke` conformance kit"},"7867":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-sparse-segment-and-volume-persistence","content":"The `sparse` segment and volume persistence"},"7868":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-latest-tag-and-registry-synchronization","content":"The `latest` tag and registry synchronization"},"7869":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7870":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7871":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7872":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-release-workflow-and-tag-based-publishing","content":"The publishing process is triggered by git tags matching the `v*` pattern or via manual dispatch `release.yml:12`. The workflow performs a critical transformation of the repository name into a lowercase registry path to comply with container registry requirements `release.yml:54`. The version is derived by stripping the leading 'v' from the git tag, ensuring that a tag like `v0.1.0` results in an image tagged `:0.1.0` `release.yml:50`."},"7873":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-check-gate-and-distribution-integrity","content":"To prevent the \"shipping of unchecked code,\" the `release` workflow enforces a strict order of operations. It first runs `./gradlew check` as a gate `release.yml:62`. The `ci/docker-build.sh` script is designed to respect this by explicitly running the gate before performing the `:booblik-app:installDist` task `docker-build.sh:21-27`. This ensures the image is built from a distribution that has already passed all tests, rather than building from a potentially broken local workspace `docker-build.sh:5-7`."},"7874":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-docker-smoke-conformance-kit","content":"The `ci/docker-smoke.sh` script performs deep inspection of the running container to ensure the runtime environment matches the measured profile. It validates that the process runs as the `booblik` user rather than `root` `docker-smoke.sh:95`. Crucially, it parses the `jvm:` line in the logs to verify that six specific flags—including `-Xmx64M` and `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=32M`—are present `docker-smoke.sh:76-83`. It also verifies that the broker's health check responds to `METADATA` requests `docker-smoke.sh:87`."},"7875":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-sparse-segment-and-volume-persistence","content":"A specific edge case is tested to ensure that the storage engine's performance characteristics are preserved in the image. The smoke test checks that when a segment is created, it remains \"sparse\" on the filesystem `docker-smoke.sh:104`. It does this by inspecting the `stat` output of the segment file to ensure the allocated blocks are significantly lower than the apparent file size `docker-smoke.sh:102-104`."},"7876":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-latest-tag-and-registry-synchronization","content":"Once the image is verified, the workflow performs a dual-push to GHCR. It tags the specific versioned image (e.g., `:0.1.0`) and the `:latest` pointer `release.yml:87-89`. This ensures that users can always pull the most recent stable version via the `:latest` tag while maintaining a permanent record of specific releases `release.yml:88`."},"7877":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"File"},"7878":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-15","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7879":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-16","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7880":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-17","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/release.yml`"},"7881":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-18","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`10-23`"},"7882":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-19","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Workflow trigger conditions and manual input definitions"},"7883":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-20","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/release.yml`"},"7884":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-21","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`39-58`"},"7885":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-22","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Version extraction logic and registry path formatting"},"7886":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-23","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`ci/docker-smoke.sh`"},"7887":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-24","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`76-83`"},"7888":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-25","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"JVM runtime profile flag verification"},"7889":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-26","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`ci/docker-build.sh`"},"7890":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-27","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`21-26`"},"7891":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-28","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Gate execution logic"},"7892":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-29","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`README.md`"},"7893":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-30","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`69-72`"},"7894":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-31","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"The specific JVM profile required for measurement"},"7895":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-32","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `pipefail` trap**: In `docker-smoke.sh:39`, using `grep` in a pipeline with `set -o pipefail` can cause a successful match to return a failure code (141) because `grep` exits early and sends a `SIGPIPE` to `docker logs`."},"7896":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-33","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `installDist` dependency**: The `ci/docker-build.sh` script does not use a multi-stage Dockerfile; instead, it relies on the host to run `:booblik-app:installDist` first to ensure the image contains the exact distribution that passed the gate `docker-build.sh:27-32`."},"7897":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","type":"page","content":"Docker image verification and conformance","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki",".github"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification"},"7898":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-0","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7899":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-1","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#the-booblikci-image-and-the-six-profile-flags","content":"The `booblik:ci` image and the six profile flags"},"7900":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-2","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#the-booblik_skip_gate-mechanism","content":"The `BOOBLIK_SKIP_GATE` mechanism"},"7901":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-3","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#the-conformancerunsh-kit","content":"The `conformance/run.sh` kit"},"7902":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-4","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#the-cidocker-smokesh-health-check","content":"The `ci/docker-smoke.sh` health check"},"7903":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-5","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7904":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-6","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7905":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-7","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7906":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-8","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#the-booblikci-image-and-the-six-profile-flags","content":"Verification of the runtime environment and the baked-in startup profile. The image is not just a wrapper for the JAR; it contains a specific set of six profile flags baked into the start script. As noted in `build.yml:58-61`, this is critical because a single `JAVA_OPTS` line in a user's `Dockerfile` could silently replace these flags, resulting in a process that no one has actually measured."},"7907":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-9","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#the-booblik_skip_gate-mechanism","content":"The lifecycle of the image build process and how it avoids redundant JVM-side checks. To optimize CI time, the image build step skips the standard gate because the `build` workflow has already executed it on the same commit, as described in `build.yml:79-80`. This is implemented by passing `BOOBLIK_SKIP_GATE: '1'` to the build script."},"7908":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-10","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#the-conformancerunsh-kit","content":"The mechanics of the reference client testing the live broker image. This kit uses a reference client to ensure the broker adheres to the specification, specifically catching errors like a partitioner that disagrees with the protocol, which unit tests of the client alone would never detect (`client-go.yml:74-76`)."},"7909":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-11","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#the-cidocker-smokesh-health-check","content":"Verification of the container's operational state via the METADATA protocol. Unlike a simple TCP connection check, the health check asks the broker for `METADATA` to ensure the process is actually responsive and not just holding a socket open in the kernel backlog (`README.md:104-106`)."},"7910":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-12","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"File"},"7911":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-13","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"Lines"},"7912":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-14","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"What is there"},"7913":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-15","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/build.yml`"},"7914":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-16","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"`82-84`"},"7915":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-17","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"Environment variable for skipping the gate during image build"},"7916":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-18","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/build.yml`"},"7917":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-19","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"`138-140`"},"7918":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-20","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"Execution of the sample script in the `dev` directory"},"7919":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-21","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"`README.md`"},"7920":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-22","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"`121-123`"},"7921":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-23","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#key-files","content":"Description of the six clients and the conformance kit"},"7922":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-24","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `BOOBLIK_SKIP_GATE` environment variable is used to prevent paying for the same check twice during the image build phase (`build.yml:83`)."},"7923":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-25","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `conformance` check is the only way to catch a partitioner that disagrees with the specification, as unit tests are insufficient for protocol-level correctness (`client-go.yml:74-76`)."},"7924":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification-26","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-verification#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"A `HEALTHCHECK` that uses the `METADATA` protocol is required because a simple TCP connection check cannot distinguish between a live broker and a hung process (`README.md:104-106`)."},"7925":{"id":"/wiki/github","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"page","content":".github","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki",".github"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github"},"7926":{"id":"/wiki/github-0","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"7927":{"id":"/wiki/github-1","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"7928":{"id":"/wiki/github-2","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#benchmark-execution-and-reporting","content":"Benchmark execution and reporting"},"7929":{"id":"/wiki/github-3","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#the-build-gate-and-smoke-testing","content":"The build gate and smoke testing"},"7930":{"id":"/wiki/github-4","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#docker-image-verification-and-conformance","content":"Docker image verification and conformance"},"7931":{"id":"/wiki/github-5","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"Client-specific gate and conformance workflows"},"7932":{"id":"/wiki/github-6","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"Key files"},"7933":{"id":"/wiki/github-7","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"7934":{"id":"/wiki/github-8","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `ef58254ca7be`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"7935":{"id":"/wiki/github-9","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `.github` module contains the Continuous Integration (CI) and Continuous Deployment (CD) workflows for the `booblik` repository. It manages the automated verification of the broker's logic, the correctness of its Docker distribution, and the compliance of various language clients against the core protocol."},"7936":{"id":"/wiki/github-10","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#benchmark-execution-and-reporting","content":"The benchmark suite is designed to detect catastrophic performance collapses rather than subtle regressions. As noted in `benchmark.yml:10-12`, a hosted runner is a different machine every time, meaning a 20% swing between runs is considered noise rather than a regression. To avoid measuring noise, the workflow runs `mainCiBenchmark` instead of `mainBenchmark` to exclude disk-bound operations like `GroupCommitBenchmark` (`benchmark.yml:22-25`). The suite runs weekly on a schedule (`benchmark.yml:36`) and uses a \"floor\" mechanism to fail only if performance drops by an order of magnitude (`benchmark.yml:74-75`)."},"7937":{"id":"/wiki/github-11","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#benchmark-execution-and-reporting","content":"More: Benchmark execution and reporting"},"7938":{"id":"/wiki/github-12","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#the-build-gate-and-smoke-testing","content":"The `build.yml` workflow implements a multi-stage gate to ensure the stability of the JVM implementation. It first executes a `check` job that includes `ktlint` and unit tests via `./ci/gate.sh jvm` (`build.yml:35-36`). Crucially, benchmarks are compiled but never executed during this stage to prevent silent rot (`build.yml:42-43`). Once the JVM logic is verified, the workflow performs a smoke test on the packaged broker using `./ci/smoke.sh` (`build.yml:47-48`) to ensure the distribution is functional."},"7939":{"id":"/wiki/github-13","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#docker-image-verification-and-conformance","content":"To ensure the broker can be shipped as a container, the `image` job in `build.yml:65-68` builds a Docker image and runs `./ci/docker-smoke.sh` (`build.yml:89`). This is followed by a conformance check where the image is tested against a reference client (`build.yml:99-102`). This process is repeated for each client workflow to ensure that the published image and the client implementations remain in sync with the protocol."},"7940":{"id":"/wiki/github-14","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#docker-image-verification-and-conformance","content":"More: Docker image verification and conformance"},"7941":{"id":"/wiki/github-15","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"Each client has a dedicated workflow to manage its specific toolchain and platform requirements:"},"7942":{"id":"/wiki/github-16","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"Client"},"7943":{"id":"/wiki/github-17","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"Workflow"},"7944":{"id":"/wiki/github-18","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"Toolchain"},"7945":{"id":"/wiki/github-19","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"Platform Constraints"},"7946":{"id":"/wiki/github-20","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":".NET"},"7947":{"id":"/wiki/github-21","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"`client-dotnet.yml:31-34`"},"7948":{"id":"/wiki/github-22","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":".NET 8.0.x"},"7949":{"id":"/wiki/github-23","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"N/A"},"7950":{"id":"/wiki/github-24","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"Go"},"7951":{"id":"/wiki/github-25","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"`client-go.yml:31-34`"},"7952":{"id":"/wiki/github-26","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"Go 1.25"},"7953":{"id":"/wiki/github-27","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#client-specific-gate-and-conformance-workflows","content":"No dependencies/cache"},"7954":{"id":"/wiki/github-28","page_id":"/wiki/github